WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.901 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.837 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:12.312 align:middle line:84%
[CLICKING] NORVIN W.
RICHARDS: So OK, problem

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:16.949 align:middle line:84%
set 0, which some of you
have already handed in-- it's

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:17.617 align:middle line:90%
not due.

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:20.353 align:middle line:84%
Don't panic if you
haven't handed it in yet.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:23.990 align:middle line:84%
It's partly a getting
to know you problem set.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.327 align:middle line:84%
We're asking you why
you're here and what

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:29.462 align:middle line:84%
you're hoping to
get out of the class

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:33.233 align:middle line:84%
so we can try to tailor the
class to your interests.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.201 align:middle line:84%
But one of the other
things we ask you to do

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:39.172 align:middle line:84%
is to get started on
the process of finding

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.341 align:middle line:84%
somebody to do
field work with, so

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:43.843 align:middle line:84%
somebody who speaks a language
that you don't speak and have

00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:45.612 align:middle line:84%
never studied, that
you're going to spend

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:48.415 align:middle line:90%
the semester harassing.

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:50.984 align:middle line:84%
So you'll be meeting with
this person every so often

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:53.620 align:middle line:84%
to ask them questions about how
to do things in their language.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.856 align:middle line:84%
And so you'll have
assignments, problem sets,

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.324 align:middle line:84%
in which we'll ask
you to find out

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:01.127 align:middle line:84%
this, or that, or the other
thing about your language.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:03.430 align:middle line:84%
So what I wanted
to start with today

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:08.768 align:middle line:84%
was to talk a little bit about
how to do that because it's not

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:09.402 align:middle line:90%
obvious.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.172 align:middle line:84%
And you'll get better at it
as the semester goes along.

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:15.742 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the reasons
we're asking you to do it.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:18.078 align:middle line:84%
So in order to talk
about how to do this,

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:22.148 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to make up a language,
sort of a fictional language.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.250 align:middle line:84%
I thought about how to
demonstrate to you how

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.251 align:middle line:90%
to do this kind of work.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.681 align:middle line:84%
And I thought about
using an actual language.

00:01:27.681 --> 00:01:30.056 align:middle line:84%
But of course, at this point,
I don't know what languages

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:31.424 align:middle line:90%
you folks are going to work on.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.259 align:middle line:84%
And so there's a danger
that I might accidentally

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:36.229 align:middle line:84%
use a language that
somebody is using.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:39.566 align:middle line:84%
So instead, I'm going
to work on Martian.

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:43.069 align:middle line:84%
So pretend that I have found
a Martian to interview.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:46.106 align:middle line:84%
And we're doing
Martian fieldwork.

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.908 align:middle line:84%
The first thing I
like to do if I'm

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.509 align:middle line:84%
working with an
unfamiliar language

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:54.372 align:middle line:84%
is to ask the person
to-- or the alien,

00:01:54.372 --> 00:01:58.051 align:middle line:84%
if it's an alien--
to give me a name.

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:01.421 align:middle line:84%
That's partly to make
it simpler for you.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:02.922 align:middle line:84%
Pretty soon, you're
going to move on

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:04.624 align:middle line:90%
to asking about sentences.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.860 align:middle line:84%
And if the sentences
have a name in them, then

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.695 align:middle line:84%
at least you know that
word in the sentence.

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:12.532 align:middle line:84%
That wipes out one piece
of the puzzle for you.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:16.002 align:middle line:84%
So I asked my Martian for
a popular Martian name,

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:18.771 align:middle line:90%
and he gave me the name X!oo.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.607 align:middle line:84%
Now, we haven't yet
talked about how

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:23.076 align:middle line:90%
to transcribe strange sounds.

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:26.540 align:middle line:84%
So when you are writing down
things in your language,

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.248 align:middle line:84%
we'll talk about this
on the problem set,

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:30.917 align:middle line:84%
but there are at least
two things you can do.

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:35.221 align:middle line:84%
If your language is standardly
written in the Roman alphabet,

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.856 align:middle line:84%
then you should feel
free to just write

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:40.493 align:middle line:84%
things in the alphabet
of the language.

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:42.428 align:middle line:84%
So if you're working
with a language that

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:44.597 align:middle line:84%
uses the Roman alphabet,
just go ahead and use it.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:46.099 align:middle line:84%
If you're working
with language that

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:50.470 align:middle line:84%
doesn't use the Roman alphabet,
then you can make something up.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.071 align:middle line:84%
And you can ask
the person you're

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:53.373 align:middle line:84%
working with if
there's a standard way

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.290 align:middle line:84%
to write their language
in the Roman alphabet.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.011 align:middle line:84%
That's sometimes
something that happens.

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.940 align:middle line:84%
But if not-- so in this
example, the Martians

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.148 align:middle line:90%
don't use the Roman alphabet.

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:03.283 align:middle line:90%
I asked the guy for a name.

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:03.950 align:middle line:90%
He said, "X!oo."

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.786 align:middle line:84%
I was, like, OK, I'll
use X, exclamation point

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:08.421 align:middle line:84%
for that clicky noise
at the beginning.

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:09.822 align:middle line:90%
And we'll go on.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.357 align:middle line:84%
Later, as I said,
we'll be talking

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.893 align:middle line:84%
about how to think
about how to write

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:17.864 align:middle line:84%
strange sounds,
unfamiliar sounds,

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:19.999 align:middle line:84%
sounds that aren't
contained in the languages

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.901 align:middle line:84%
that you're familiar
with, maybe.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.070 align:middle line:84%
So we'll be talking about
the International Phonetic

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.874 align:middle line:84%
Alphabet, which is a system
for transcribing speech sounds.

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:29.876 align:middle line:84%
But for now, since we
haven't done that yet,

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:31.211 align:middle line:90%
we'll just make stuff up so.

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:35.582 align:middle line:90%
There's a Martian name, X!oo.

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:40.553 align:middle line:84%
And then, you get
started on sentences.

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:43.022 align:middle line:84%
And you might want to start
with simple sentences,

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:44.156 align:middle line:90%
like "X!oo is a linguist."

00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:50.697 align:middle line:84%
I got that one for Martian,
"X!oo kuulduud bii."

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:52.165 align:middle line:90%
And that's a sentence.

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.667 align:middle line:84%
I asked my Martian, how many
words are in that sentence?

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.402 align:middle line:84%
And they told me
there are three.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:04:01.073 align:middle line:84%
And you might wonder--
well, I started with a name

00:04:01.073 --> 00:04:02.942 align:middle line:90%
partly to make my life simpler.

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:04.544 align:middle line:84%
I know what the
first word means,

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:06.412 align:middle line:84%
but what do the second
and third words mean?

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.381 align:middle line:84%
And there are various
ways to do that as you're

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:10.717 align:middle line:84%
working with the person
that you're working with.

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:12.485 align:middle line:84%
You could just
straight up ask them

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:16.789 align:middle line:84%
things like, "Which word in
here is the word for "linguist?"

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:18.925 align:middle line:84%
You could just say, "What's
the word for linguist?"

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.827 align:middle line:84%
Or you could ask for
another sentence that's

00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:23.529 align:middle line:84%
minimally different
from this one,

00:04:23.529 --> 00:04:25.465 align:middle line:84%
just in, say, the
word for linguist.

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:29.602 align:middle line:84%
So ask, "How do you say
X!oo is a physicist?"

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:30.970 align:middle line:90%
So you get that.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.880 align:middle line:84%
You discover that this sentence
consists of three words,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.028 align:middle line:84%
"X!oo," and then the word for
linguist, which is "kuulduud,"

00:04:38.028 --> 00:04:41.814 align:middle line:84%
and the word for
is, which is "bii."

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:44.350 align:middle line:84%
What I've done
here is to present

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.654 align:middle line:84%
this piece of data, this
sentence with the three words

00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:50.556 align:middle line:84%
that have these three meanings,
in the way that we're going

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.091 align:middle line:90%
to ask you to present the data.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.360 align:middle line:84%
And we'll again, on
the first problem set,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.728 align:middle line:90%
will reiterate this.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:59.365 align:middle line:84%
But this is the standard way you
represent data in linguistics.

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:03.002 align:middle line:84%
You'll have a sentence in
the language of interest.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:06.406 align:middle line:84%
And under that sentence,
under each word,

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:08.675 align:middle line:84%
you'll have a translation
for that word.

00:05:08.675 --> 00:05:10.443 align:middle line:90%
So that's called the gloss.

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:14.580 align:middle line:84%
So the gloss for "kuulduud"
here is "linguist."

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.082 align:middle line:84%
And then on the third line,
you have a translation

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:19.185 align:middle line:84%
of the entire
sentence into English.

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:22.188 align:middle line:84%
There's more to say, but for now
that's what you need to know.

00:05:22.188 --> 00:05:23.656 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.491 align:middle line:84%
One of the things
you do by doing it

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.860 align:middle line:84%
this way is it's an easy
way to get across the fact

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.296 align:middle line:84%
that the word order of Martian
is not the same as the word

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:32.932 align:middle line:84%
order of English, since
the Martian sentence ends

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:35.168 align:middle line:90%
with the Martian word for "is."

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:39.839 align:middle line:84%
We're learning something
about Martian word order.

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:43.843 align:middle line:84%
Moving on, you get some
more Martian sentences.

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:46.612 align:middle line:84%
"X!oo amsterdam dig dug,"
so "X!oo dug a canal,"

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:49.382 align:middle line:84%
"X!oo is digging a
canal," and so on.

00:05:49.382 --> 00:05:54.120 align:middle line:84%
So just depending on what
we've asked you to find out,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.422 align:middle line:84%
you that's going to
guide what particular

00:05:56.422 --> 00:05:58.358 align:middle line:90%
sentences you might ask for.

00:05:58.358 --> 00:05:59.759 align:middle line:84%
Probably what
we'll do is ask you

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:01.828 align:middle line:84%
to get some basic
sentences in the language.

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:04.530 align:middle line:84%
And you'll want to have some
sentences that have subjects

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:08.301 align:middle line:84%
and objects, like
"X!oo dug a canal."

00:06:08.301 --> 00:06:10.103 align:middle line:84%
One of the first things
we'll ask you to do

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:13.439 align:middle line:84%
is to find out how
to negate sentences,

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:16.442 align:middle line:84%
so how to say that
something is not true.

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:19.312 align:middle line:84%
So in this particular
case, what I

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:21.114 align:middle line:84%
did was to get three
Martian sentences,

00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:23.649 align:middle line:84%
and then to get minimally
different sentences

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:25.318 align:middle line:90%
in which we negated things.

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:27.787 align:middle line:84%
So we're not going to
say "X!oo is a linguist,"

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:29.222 align:middle line:84%
we'll say "X!oo
isn't a linguist."

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:31.391 align:middle line:84%
And that changes it
from "X!oo kuulduud bii"

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:35.395 align:middle line:90%
to "X!oo kuulduud noowee."

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:39.604 align:middle line:84%
Or the way you do "X!oo
didn't dig a canal"

00:06:39.604 --> 00:06:42.435 align:middle line:90%
is "X!oo amsterdam digwedug."

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:45.405 align:middle line:84%
So we've changed the verb
"digdug," which means "dug,"

00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:46.272 align:middle line:90%
to "digwedug."

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:48.908 align:middle line:90%
That makes it negative.

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:52.111 align:middle line:84%
So this is a way of
gathering, bit by bit,

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.880 align:middle line:90%
bits of Martian data.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.216 align:middle line:84%
So in particular here, we're
interested in Martian negation.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:59.085 align:middle line:84%
And we're finding
things out about how

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:04.357 align:middle line:90%
negation is expressed.

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:08.795 align:middle line:84%
So a hypothesis I came up with
on the basis of these facts

00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:13.533 align:middle line:84%
looks as though the negative
of "is" is just irregular.

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:17.336 align:middle line:84%
So "bii" is "is," but
"noowee" is "is not."

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:19.772 align:middle line:84%
Those don't look related
to each other at all.

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:22.175 align:middle line:84%
That just looks like an
irregular correspondence,

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:28.648 align:middle line:84%
but that for "dig" changed
to "digwedug," and "gudgid"

00:07:28.648 --> 00:07:30.082 align:middle line:90%
changed to "gudwegid."

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:33.352 align:middle line:84%
And that makes me
think, there's a "we"

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:36.389 align:middle line:84%
that goes in the middle
of those words somewhere.

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:39.158 align:middle line:84%
So for "digdug," we got
"digwedug," and for "gudgid,"

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:41.127 align:middle line:90%
we got "gudwegid."

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.429 align:middle line:84%
And at this point, we might
have a number of hypotheses

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:45.631 align:middle line:90%
about where "we" goes.

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.066 align:middle line:84%
I've put a couple
of them up here.

00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:50.236 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, I should have made you
guys generate the hypotheses.

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:52.939 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we could think that "we"
goes after the first syllable,

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:55.408 align:middle line:84%
so you start with "digdug"
and you get "digwedug."

00:07:55.408 --> 00:07:57.844 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe that it goes
before the last syllable,

00:07:57.844 --> 00:07:59.979 align:middle line:84%
so you start with "digdug,"
and you get "digwedug."

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:02.909 align:middle line:84%
That covers those
two bits of data.

00:08:02.909 --> 00:08:04.617 align:middle line:84%
Anybody have another
hypothesis they want

00:08:04.617 --> 00:08:08.154 align:middle line:90%
to float about where "we" goes?

00:08:08.154 --> 00:08:10.723 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:16.290 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I'm not actually sure--
did you just completely reverse

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:17.396 align:middle line:90%
the order?

00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:20.132 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah, to
get from "digdug" to "gudgid,"

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:21.968 align:middle line:84%
yeah, to get from the
past into the present.

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:23.936 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:08:23.936 --> 00:08:25.605 align:middle line:84%
But I'm asking you
to give me hypotheses

00:08:25.605 --> 00:08:26.839 align:middle line:90%
about negation right now.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:27.473 align:middle line:90%
But yes, you're right.

00:08:27.473 --> 00:08:29.242 align:middle line:84%
That's a hypothesis
about how tense works.

00:08:29.242 --> 00:08:31.310 align:middle line:84%
The tense involves
saying words backwards.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:32.278 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:34.447 align:middle line:84%
And we would want to find
out whether that was true.

00:08:34.447 --> 00:08:36.048 align:middle line:84%
It would be exciting
if that were true

00:08:36.048 --> 00:08:38.484 align:middle line:84%
because there are not human
languages that work that way.

00:08:38.484 --> 00:08:42.522 align:middle line:84%
But of course, this is Martian,
so all bets are off, I guess.

00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:43.523 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:43.523 --> 00:08:46.025 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: In the exact
middle of the word.

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:48.861 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah,
so count letters, and these

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:50.696 align:middle line:84%
are six-letter words,
and so you put "we"

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:52.431 align:middle line:90%
between letters three and four.

00:08:52.431 --> 00:08:55.101 align:middle line:90%
It could be that.

00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:56.569 align:middle line:84%
Any theories people
want to push?

00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:57.069 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:57.069 --> 00:09:01.207 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: It could also be
like in between syllables.

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:03.109 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:04.544 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: What is it that's--

00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:10.049 align:middle line:84%
I would ask-- in that
case, I would ask for--

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:12.812 align:middle line:84%
I would try to find a
six-letter word that

00:09:12.812 --> 00:09:13.979 align:middle line:90%
has-- that looks like a 4-2.

00:09:13.979 --> 00:09:15.895 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah,
I see what you mean.

00:09:15.895 --> 00:09:17.557 align:middle line:84%
So I just said you're
counting letters,

00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:19.559 align:middle line:84%
but you're absolutely right--
we could be counting syllables.

00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:21.093 align:middle line:84%
These are two-syllable
words, and you put it

00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:22.495 align:middle line:84%
between the first and
the second syllable.

00:09:22.495 --> 00:09:23.763 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's absolutely right.

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:26.699 align:middle line:84%
You guys have collaborated on
an excellent hypothesis there.

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:29.335 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, another thing?

00:09:29.335 --> 00:09:30.703 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:30.703 --> 00:09:32.605 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: You're also
using the same verb

00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:33.906 align:middle line:90%
for both of these situations.

00:09:33.906 --> 00:09:35.485 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:09:35.485 --> 00:09:36.943 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Could be, yeah.

00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:40.613 align:middle line:84%
So I took the
flying leap of faith

00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:42.848 align:middle line:84%
that yeah, there was
something special about "bii."

00:09:42.848 --> 00:09:45.503 align:middle line:84%
But maybe "dig" is
something that's

00:09:45.503 --> 00:09:47.086 align:middle line:84%
going to illustrate
a general pattern,

00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:48.254 align:middle line:90%
but you're absolutely right.

00:09:48.254 --> 00:09:49.956 align:middle line:90%
Could be this is also irregular.

00:09:49.956 --> 00:09:51.790 align:middle line:84%
Clearly what we
need is more verbs.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:53.259 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:57.530 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Language [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.465 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Oh,
that's a nice thought.

00:09:59.465 --> 00:10:01.567 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, maybe "we"
goes after the stress

00:10:01.567 --> 00:10:02.602 align:middle line:90%
or before the stress.

00:10:02.602 --> 00:10:04.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's cool.

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:06.205 align:middle line:84%
My Martian accent
is not so great.

00:10:06.205 --> 00:10:07.873 align:middle line:84%
So we haven't been
able to work on that.

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:10.242 align:middle line:90%
But yeah, nice.

00:10:10.242 --> 00:10:11.477 align:middle line:90%
These are nice ideas.

00:10:11.477 --> 00:10:13.907 align:middle line:84%
So what we need to do
is find more words.

00:10:13.907 --> 00:10:16.115 align:middle line:84%
How, for example, are we
going to distinguish the two

00:10:16.115 --> 00:10:18.012 align:middle line:84%
hypotheses that I
have on the board

00:10:18.012 --> 00:10:19.819 align:middle line:84%
here-- so after
the first syllable

00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:21.354 align:middle line:90%
or before the last syllable?

00:10:21.354 --> 00:10:22.221 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:22.221 --> 00:10:23.522 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I want to get a
word with three syllables.

00:10:23.522 --> 00:10:25.224 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah, I
want to get a longer word.

00:10:25.224 --> 00:10:26.292 align:middle line:84%
So if we have a
longer, word we'll

00:10:26.292 --> 00:10:27.360 align:middle line:84%
be able to find
out what that is.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:28.461 align:middle line:90%
This was your point, too.

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:30.889 align:middle line:84%
If we have a word where one
syllable is really long,

00:10:30.889 --> 00:10:32.264 align:middle line:84%
another syllable
is really short,

00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:35.067 align:middle line:84%
maybe we can learn
something by doing that,

00:10:35.067 --> 00:10:38.337 align:middle line:90%
so get some more words.

00:10:38.337 --> 00:10:42.208 align:middle line:84%
Here's "X!oo is singing,"
"X!oo yodeleehihuu,"

00:10:42.208 --> 00:10:45.611 align:middle line:84%
and "X!oo destroyed
a spacecraft,"

00:10:45.611 --> 00:10:48.314 align:middle line:90%
"X!oo roovaa munchmunchyum."

00:10:48.314 --> 00:10:51.350 align:middle line:84%
So here are two verbs
that are longer.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:54.553 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll be able to tell,
by negating these, which

00:10:54.553 --> 00:10:56.756 align:middle line:84%
of the various hypotheses
we've been floating

00:10:56.756 --> 00:10:59.725 align:middle line:90%
are the most attractive ones.

00:10:59.725 --> 00:11:06.732 align:middle line:84%
Turns out we get
"yowedeleehihuu" and

00:11:06.732 --> 00:11:08.100 align:middle line:90%
"munchwemunchyum."

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:11.971 align:middle line:84%
So you're right, I'm
not saying it right.

00:11:11.971 --> 00:11:14.106 align:middle line:90%
So what's the rule?

00:11:14.106 --> 00:11:15.408 align:middle line:90%
Where does "we" go?

00:11:15.408 --> 00:11:17.076 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: After the
first consonant.

00:11:17.076 --> 00:11:19.512 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: After the
first consonant, looks like.

00:11:19.512 --> 00:11:21.113 align:middle line:84%
Or if stress is on
the first syllable,

00:11:21.113 --> 00:11:22.581 align:middle line:84%
it could still be
after the stress.

00:11:22.581 --> 00:11:23.916 align:middle line:90%
We have to study that.

00:11:23.916 --> 00:11:25.110 align:middle line:90%
That's absolutely right.

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:26.652 align:middle line:84%
So now, we have a
working hypothesis.

00:11:26.652 --> 00:11:29.088 align:middle line:84%
We could go get more verbs
and test the hypothesis

00:11:29.088 --> 00:11:32.224 align:middle line:84%
against-- if we could
find a verb that had maybe

00:11:32.224 --> 00:11:34.627 align:middle line:84%
all of these have initial
stress, and if we work harder,

00:11:34.627 --> 00:11:36.328 align:middle line:84%
we'll find a verb
that has final stress.

00:11:36.328 --> 00:11:38.631 align:middle line:84%
And we'll learn something
about whether stress matters.

00:11:38.631 --> 00:11:41.300 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, basically, I'm
asking you to do science.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:44.537 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to gather
data, generate a hypothesis

00:11:44.537 --> 00:11:47.206 align:middle line:84%
about the data,
realize that there

00:11:47.206 --> 00:11:49.642 align:middle line:84%
are multiple hypotheses that
are compatible with the data,

00:11:49.642 --> 00:11:51.110 align:middle line:84%
try to figure out
what kind of data

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.612 align:middle line:84%
you'd like to have that allow
you to test your hypothesis

00:11:53.612 --> 00:11:56.682 align:middle line:90%
further, go gather those data.

00:11:56.682 --> 00:11:57.917 align:middle line:90%
That's the idea.

00:11:57.917 --> 00:12:00.019 align:middle line:90%
Any questions about that so far?

00:12:00.019 --> 00:12:06.492 align:middle line:90%
That's the goal.

00:12:06.492 --> 00:12:09.732 align:middle line:84%
A couple of general
things-- maybe

00:12:09.732 --> 00:12:11.163 align:middle line:84%
I should start
with the last one.

00:12:11.163 --> 00:12:13.532 align:middle line:84%
You should be very nice
to this person, the person

00:12:13.532 --> 00:12:16.068 align:middle line:90%
that you're working with.

00:12:16.068 --> 00:12:19.271 align:middle line:84%
We're asking you to
do some work for you.

00:12:19.271 --> 00:12:21.807 align:middle line:84%
But the Linguistics Department
isn't offering to pay them,

00:12:21.807 --> 00:12:23.209 align:middle line:90%
for example.

00:12:23.209 --> 00:12:25.211 align:middle line:84%
We're just hoping that
you will be nice to them.

00:12:25.211 --> 00:12:27.713 align:middle line:84%
So we encourage
you to treat them

00:12:27.713 --> 00:12:31.450 align:middle line:84%
the way you would treat anybody
who is helping you a lot, which

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:33.545 align:middle line:90%
hopefully, is nicely.

00:12:33.545 --> 00:12:35.087 align:middle line:84%
Find out what their
favorite food is.

00:12:35.087 --> 00:12:37.123 align:middle line:90%
Bring it to them.

00:12:37.123 --> 00:12:39.525 align:middle line:84%
Offer to be nice to them
in whatever way they need

00:12:39.525 --> 00:12:42.228 align:middle line:90%
niceness.

00:12:42.228 --> 00:12:44.263 align:middle line:84%
It's maybe important
to emphasize that,

00:12:44.263 --> 00:12:47.833 align:middle line:84%
not just because these people
aren't being paid, but also

00:12:47.833 --> 00:12:50.770 align:middle line:84%
because this is
something I have-- see,

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:54.373 align:middle line:84%
I teach a Field Methods
class for graduate students.

00:12:54.373 --> 00:12:56.809 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of
work, where you're

00:12:56.809 --> 00:12:59.845 align:middle line:84%
working with a human
being, is pretty different

00:12:59.845 --> 00:13:03.467 align:middle line:84%
from, well, a lot of the kinds
of scientific work that you do,

00:13:03.467 --> 00:13:05.384 align:middle line:84%
and even a lot of the
kinds of scientific work

00:13:05.384 --> 00:13:06.137 align:middle line:90%
that linguists do.

00:13:06.137 --> 00:13:08.220 align:middle line:84%
Often, when we're gathering
data about a language,

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.855 align:middle line:84%
we're doing it by
reading your grammar,

00:13:09.855 --> 00:13:12.158 align:middle line:84%
or by talking to
a linguist, which

00:13:12.158 --> 00:13:15.628 align:middle line:84%
is not quite the same as
talking to a human being.

00:13:15.628 --> 00:13:18.631 align:middle line:84%
Linguists-- we can
take shortcuts.

00:13:18.631 --> 00:13:21.300 align:middle line:84%
We know how to talk to each
other in linguistic code

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:23.903 align:middle line:84%
and to get quickly to
what it is that we want.

00:13:23.903 --> 00:13:26.138 align:middle line:84%
If you're talking to
a normal human being,

00:13:26.138 --> 00:13:29.175 align:middle line:84%
it's especially
important to be nice,

00:13:29.175 --> 00:13:31.143 align:middle line:90%
to remind yourself to be nice.

00:13:31.143 --> 00:13:32.945 align:middle line:84%
Because it's easy
to find yourself

00:13:32.945 --> 00:13:36.315 align:middle line:84%
irritated with this person when
they disprove your hypotheses.

00:13:36.315 --> 00:13:41.420 align:middle line:84%
So if you've got a hypothesis,
and the person then gives you

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:43.756 align:middle line:84%
data that disprove your
hypothesis-- it's something

00:13:43.756 --> 00:13:45.524 align:middle line:90%
I often see people do in class.

00:13:45.524 --> 00:13:47.893 align:middle line:84%
They try to talk the
person out of it.

00:13:47.893 --> 00:13:49.295 align:middle line:90%
They're, like, "No, wait.

00:13:49.295 --> 00:13:50.129 align:middle line:90%
Are you sure?"

00:13:50.129 --> 00:13:52.231 align:middle line:90%
So don't do that.

00:13:52.231 --> 00:13:53.599 align:middle line:90%
Just gather the data.

00:13:53.599 --> 00:13:56.836 align:middle line:84%
Don't get emotionally
attached to your hypotheses.

00:13:56.836 --> 00:13:58.871 align:middle line:84%
Be happy when you see
your hypotheses die

00:13:58.871 --> 00:14:03.409 align:middle line:84%
and get replaced by
other hypotheses.

00:14:03.409 --> 00:14:04.927 align:middle line:84%
You should try--
I guess I'm going

00:14:04.927 --> 00:14:07.713 align:middle line:84%
through this list backwards--
you should try to be organized.

00:14:07.713 --> 00:14:11.684 align:middle line:84%
And by that what I mean
is this kind of work,

00:14:11.684 --> 00:14:14.787 align:middle line:84%
it involves being fast on
your feet to a certain extent.

00:14:14.787 --> 00:14:18.490 align:middle line:84%
So when I was doing that
first bit of Martian data,

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:22.027 align:middle line:84%
it's like, OK, there's a
"we" that indicates negation.

00:14:22.027 --> 00:14:24.163 align:middle line:84%
And it's going in the middle
of the verb somewhere.

00:14:24.163 --> 00:14:26.498 align:middle line:84%
But what exactly do I mean
by "middle of the verb?"

00:14:26.498 --> 00:14:28.667 align:middle line:84%
And then we were generating
various hypotheses

00:14:28.667 --> 00:14:29.628 align:middle line:90%
about what that meant.

00:14:29.628 --> 00:14:31.170 align:middle line:84%
And that makes it
clear what you need

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:34.206 align:middle line:84%
to do next, which is
to get more verbs.

00:14:34.206 --> 00:14:36.040 align:middle line:84%
That's something
that you discover

00:14:36.040 --> 00:14:37.276 align:middle line:90%
in the middle of the session.

00:14:37.276 --> 00:14:38.744 align:middle line:90%
You find out how negation works.

00:14:38.744 --> 00:14:41.280 align:middle line:84%
Oh, it goes in the
middle of the verb.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.816 align:middle line:84%
Then what I need is more verbs
so that I can define middle

00:14:43.816 --> 00:14:46.318 align:middle line:90%
in the appropriate way.

00:14:46.318 --> 00:14:50.222 align:middle line:84%
So you want to go into
the session with a plan

00:14:50.222 --> 00:14:52.424 align:middle line:84%
for what exactly you're
going to try to find out

00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:54.393 align:middle line:90%
and in what order.

00:14:54.393 --> 00:15:01.333 align:middle line:84%
And then two things-- my plans
often look like flowcharts,

00:15:01.333 --> 00:15:04.103 align:middle line:84%
or choose your own adventure
novels, or something like that.

00:15:04.103 --> 00:15:06.505 align:middle line:84%
It's like I will ask this,
and if they say yes, then I

00:15:06.505 --> 00:15:07.082 align:middle line:90%
go over here.

00:15:07.082 --> 00:15:08.540 align:middle line:84%
And if they say
no, I go over here.

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:10.910 align:middle line:84%
If they say no to that,
well, then we go over here.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:15.514 align:middle line:84%
So you make yourself
a set of ways

00:15:15.514 --> 00:15:19.370 align:middle line:84%
of dealing with various possible
things that they might say.

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:20.953 align:middle line:84%
and then the other
thing you do is not

00:15:20.953 --> 00:15:22.655 align:middle line:84%
get too attached
to that, either.

00:15:22.655 --> 00:15:25.958 align:middle line:84%
So they may say something you
didn't imagine them saying.

00:15:25.958 --> 00:15:29.161 align:middle line:84%
And you have to be ready
to think on your feet,

00:15:29.161 --> 00:15:34.233 align:middle line:84%
be ready to do whatever
it is that they say.

00:15:34.233 --> 00:15:36.035 align:middle line:84%
So when I say be
organized, what I mean is,

00:15:36.035 --> 00:15:39.305 align:middle line:84%
think ahead about what you will
do under various circumstances.

00:15:39.305 --> 00:15:42.802 align:middle line:84%
But then also, be
quick thinking.

00:15:42.802 --> 00:15:44.510 align:middle line:84%
And that's something
you'll get better at

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:48.814 align:middle line:90%
as you do more and more of it.

00:15:48.814 --> 00:15:51.650 align:middle line:84%
Don't assume that you're
getting what you're asking for.

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:54.453 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean by that?

00:15:54.453 --> 00:15:57.423 align:middle line:84%
I've done some field work on a
language called Lardil, which

00:15:57.423 --> 00:16:01.327 align:middle line:84%
is an Aboriginal language spoken
on Mornington Island, which

00:16:01.327 --> 00:16:03.929 align:middle line:84%
is a beautiful island off the
northern coast of Australia.

00:16:03.929 --> 00:16:06.031 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at
the map of Australia,

00:16:06.031 --> 00:16:09.034 align:middle line:84%
there's a big dent in
the northern coast.

00:16:09.034 --> 00:16:11.570 align:middle line:84%
That's the Gulf of Carpentaria,
and Mornington Island

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:13.072 align:middle line:90%
is in the middle of that.

00:16:13.072 --> 00:16:15.207 align:middle line:84%
Lardil is spoken by Aborigines
who have lived there

00:16:15.207 --> 00:16:19.278 align:middle line:90%
for thousands of years.

00:16:19.278 --> 00:16:25.551 align:middle line:84%
I was once walking with a Lardil
speaker and talking with him.

00:16:25.551 --> 00:16:30.315 align:middle line:84%
And we walked by a truck that
was parked in front of a house.

00:16:30.315 --> 00:16:31.857 align:middle line:84%
And I asked him,
hey, how do you say,

00:16:31.857 --> 00:16:33.359 align:middle line:84%
"The truck is in
front of the house?"

00:16:33.359 --> 00:16:34.760 align:middle line:84%
And he thought about
that for a second,

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.495 align:middle line:84%
and he said something
to me in Lardil

00:16:36.495 --> 00:16:40.532 align:middle line:84%
which meant, "The truck is
to the south of the house,"

00:16:40.532 --> 00:16:42.334 align:middle line:84%
because in Lardil,
that's what you do.

00:16:42.334 --> 00:16:44.670 align:middle line:84%
You talk about spatial
relations entirely in terms

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.305 align:middle line:90%
of cardinal directions.

00:16:46.305 --> 00:16:48.368 align:middle line:84%
It's not that Lardil
doesn't have a word for it

00:16:48.368 --> 00:16:50.743 align:middle line:84%
"in front of," but it would
have been unnatural in Lardil

00:16:50.743 --> 00:16:53.479 align:middle line:84%
to say, "The truck is
in front of the house."

00:16:53.479 --> 00:16:55.914 align:middle line:84%
So he didn't answer my
question, in a sense.

00:16:55.914 --> 00:16:57.383 align:middle line:90%
And in another sense, he did.

00:16:57.383 --> 00:16:58.951 align:middle line:84%
He told me what a
Lardil person would

00:16:58.951 --> 00:17:02.121 align:middle line:84%
say to describe what I
wanted them to describe.

00:17:02.121 --> 00:17:03.555 align:middle line:90%
That may happen to you.

00:17:03.555 --> 00:17:05.550 align:middle line:84%
So you may ask somebody
how to say something,

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:07.092 align:middle line:84%
and they may tell
you something which

00:17:07.092 --> 00:17:09.627 align:middle line:90%
is not exactly what you meant.

00:17:09.627 --> 00:17:12.798 align:middle line:84%
There sort of isn't any
way to prepare for that.

00:17:12.798 --> 00:17:14.599 align:middle line:84%
You just have to do
the best you can.

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:17.169 align:middle line:84%
So find out everything about
the parts of what they've asked.

00:17:17.169 --> 00:17:20.705 align:middle line:84%
So as we started off, I
said, "X!oo kuulduud bii."

00:17:20.705 --> 00:17:22.340 align:middle line:84%
That means, "X!oo
is a linguist."

00:17:22.340 --> 00:17:24.309 align:middle line:84%
And then you do some
work to find out

00:17:24.309 --> 00:17:25.711 align:middle line:90%
what does each word mean.

00:17:25.711 --> 00:17:27.479 align:middle line:84%
And as you do that
work, you may be

00:17:27.479 --> 00:17:29.548 align:middle line:84%
able to avoid some of
these kinds of problems.

00:17:29.548 --> 00:17:33.752 align:middle line:84%
So there's a
recording-- oh, yeah.

00:17:33.752 --> 00:17:37.589 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What if-- not that they
don't necessarily have a word

00:17:37.589 --> 00:17:39.992 align:middle line:84%
for something, but it's the
English word that they just

00:17:39.992 --> 00:17:41.160 align:middle line:90%
don't have another--

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:41.727 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
You should probably

00:17:41.727 --> 00:17:42.961 align:middle line:84%
try to find
something else, then.

00:17:42.961 --> 00:17:44.003 align:middle line:90%
That's a very good point.

00:17:44.003 --> 00:17:46.932 align:middle line:84%
So you should do things that
are culturally appropriate.

00:17:46.932 --> 00:17:48.967 align:middle line:84%
I like to start,
often, by asking people

00:17:48.967 --> 00:17:50.803 align:middle line:90%
what their favorite food is.

00:17:50.803 --> 00:17:56.408 align:middle line:84%
And then, if they tell you,
"My favorite food is apples,"

00:17:56.408 --> 00:17:58.977 align:middle line:84%
then all your sentences
can be about apples.

00:17:58.977 --> 00:18:00.979 align:middle line:84%
"I'm eating apples,"
or whatever.

00:18:00.979 --> 00:18:02.281 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a very good point.

00:18:02.281 --> 00:18:03.682 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:18:03.682 --> 00:18:05.884 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: We have to
verify [INAUDIBLE]....

00:18:05.884 --> 00:18:10.015 align:middle line:84%
So for example, in your example,
if you have a hypothesis that

00:18:10.015 --> 00:18:12.558 align:middle line:84%
no goes after the
first syllable,

00:18:12.558 --> 00:18:15.594 align:middle line:84%
does it make sense
to [INAUDIBLE]????

00:18:15.594 --> 00:18:17.763 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Oh, it absolutely does.

00:18:17.763 --> 00:18:19.264 align:middle line:84%
So let me just
repeat your question.

00:18:19.264 --> 00:18:20.966 align:middle line:84%
Does it make sense to
ask the native speaker?

00:18:20.966 --> 00:18:23.102 align:middle line:84%
I've got this hypothesis,
now, about what's going on.

00:18:23.102 --> 00:18:25.144 align:middle line:84%
It looks like "we" goes
after the first syllable.

00:18:25.144 --> 00:18:27.272 align:middle line:84%
It absolutely does
make sense to ask

00:18:27.272 --> 00:18:29.208 align:middle line:90%
and it's totally fine to ask.

00:18:29.208 --> 00:18:32.244 align:middle line:84%
You shouldn't necessarily
assume that they're

00:18:32.244 --> 00:18:34.580 align:middle line:90%
right when they tell you.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:39.785 align:middle line:84%
Because-- this came up when
we were talking last week--

00:18:39.785 --> 00:18:42.287 align:middle line:84%
you know many things
about your native language

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:45.357 align:middle line:84%
that you don't have to think
about in order to speak it.

00:18:45.357 --> 00:18:48.694 align:middle line:84%
And the consequence of that
is that if somebody suddenly

00:18:48.694 --> 00:18:51.663 align:middle line:84%
asks you, hey, what's
the rule for this,

00:18:51.663 --> 00:18:53.665 align:middle line:90%
you might not actually know.

00:18:53.665 --> 00:18:55.234 align:middle line:90%
Because it's effortless for you.

00:18:55.234 --> 00:18:57.970 align:middle line:84%
And so you may get
a good response

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:59.972 align:middle line:84%
when you ask that
question and you may not.

00:18:59.972 --> 00:19:02.407 align:middle line:84%
That means that there are two
kinds of possible mistakes

00:19:02.407 --> 00:19:04.743 align:middle line:84%
that you could make when
somebody tells you their theory

00:19:04.743 --> 00:19:05.911 align:middle line:90%
about what's going on.

00:19:05.911 --> 00:19:08.580 align:middle line:84%
One is to uncritically
believe it

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.282 align:middle line:84%
because they're
a native speaker.

00:19:10.282 --> 00:19:12.017 align:middle line:84%
And the other would
be to discard it.

00:19:12.017 --> 00:19:13.418 align:middle line:90%
You see linguists do that, too.

00:19:13.418 --> 00:19:14.686 align:middle line:84%
They're, like, "I'm
the linguist here.

00:19:14.686 --> 00:19:16.355 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to
listen to this person."

00:19:16.355 --> 00:19:18.891 align:middle line:84%
That's also a mistake because
of course, their input on this

00:19:18.891 --> 00:19:19.658 align:middle line:90%
is valuable.

00:19:19.658 --> 00:19:22.094 align:middle line:84%
It's just one valuable
source of information,

00:19:22.094 --> 00:19:24.429 align:middle line:84%
which you need to weigh
against any other sources

00:19:24.429 --> 00:19:25.931 align:middle line:90%
of information that you have.

00:19:25.931 --> 00:19:27.366 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:19:27.366 --> 00:19:30.068 align:middle line:90%
It's a really good question.

00:19:30.068 --> 00:19:33.472 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, I have one other
anecdote about Lardil.

00:19:33.472 --> 00:19:37.643 align:middle line:84%
I found a recording, one of
the first recordings of Lardil,

00:19:37.643 --> 00:19:40.279 align:middle line:90%
by an aircraft pilot.

00:19:40.279 --> 00:19:41.280 align:middle line:90%
So he wasn't a linguist.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.216 align:middle line:84%
He flew regular roots
to Mornington Island,

00:19:44.216 --> 00:19:46.285 align:middle line:84%
this really isolated place,
and he got interested

00:19:46.285 --> 00:19:47.152 align:middle line:90%
in the language.

00:19:47.152 --> 00:19:50.389 align:middle line:84%
And he sat down
a Lardil speaker,

00:19:50.389 --> 00:19:52.257 align:middle line:84%
and he was going to
record a word list.

00:19:52.257 --> 00:19:54.893 align:middle line:84%
He'd gotten hold of a
list of basic vocabulary.

00:19:54.893 --> 00:19:56.795 align:middle line:84%
And so he was
going to try to get

00:19:56.795 --> 00:19:58.730 align:middle line:84%
the person to record
translations for all

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:00.399 align:middle line:90%
these basic English words.

00:20:00.399 --> 00:20:03.170 align:middle line:84%
And his list clearly
started with pronouns.

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:05.003 align:middle line:84%
So he was going to list
a bunch of pronouns.

00:20:05.003 --> 00:20:08.073 align:middle line:84%
So he started with "I," said,
tell me the word for "I."

00:20:08.073 --> 00:20:10.475 align:middle line:84%
And the guy gave him the
word for what you see with,

00:20:10.475 --> 00:20:12.411 align:middle line:90%
this kind of eye.

00:20:12.411 --> 00:20:14.980 align:middle line:84%
Then he said the
word for "thou."

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:16.648 align:middle line:84%
And there's this
pause on the tape.

00:20:16.648 --> 00:20:18.913 align:middle line:84%
You can hear the
Lardil speaking, "thou,

00:20:18.913 --> 00:20:19.885 align:middle line:90%
what the heck?"

00:20:19.885 --> 00:20:22.321 align:middle line:84%
And then he gives him the
third person singular,

00:20:22.321 --> 00:20:24.769 align:middle line:84%
the word for "he or
she," which is actually

00:20:24.769 --> 00:20:27.203 align:middle line:84%
a pretty good answer
because in Lardil, you

00:20:27.203 --> 00:20:30.702 align:middle line:84%
use the third person "he or
she" under certain cultural

00:20:30.702 --> 00:20:32.744 align:middle line:84%
circumstances, to indicate
respect for the person

00:20:32.744 --> 00:20:33.744 align:middle line:90%
that you're speaking to.

00:20:33.744 --> 00:20:35.419 align:middle line:84%
You speak to them
as though they're

00:20:35.419 --> 00:20:37.169 align:middle line:84%
in the third person
instead of the second.

00:20:37.169 --> 00:20:40.272 align:middle line:84%
So you don't say "you" to
them, you say "he" or "she."

00:20:40.272 --> 00:20:42.207 align:middle line:84%
So he gave him a
fancy word for "you,"

00:20:42.207 --> 00:20:43.642 align:middle line:84%
which is a pretty
good translation

00:20:43.642 --> 00:20:45.143 align:middle line:90%
for that whole action.

00:20:45.143 --> 00:20:47.764 align:middle line:84%
But these are just
examples of you may not get

00:20:47.764 --> 00:20:49.014 align:middle line:90%
what you ask for, necessarily.

00:20:49.014 --> 00:20:54.553 align:middle line:90%
You should be prepared for that.

00:20:54.553 --> 00:20:57.589 align:middle line:84%
Now we're up to the second
sentence on this slide.

00:20:57.589 --> 00:21:00.325 align:middle line:84%
So start with simple culturally
appropriate sentences.

00:21:00.325 --> 00:21:02.127 align:middle line:84%
This is kind of what
you were asking about.

00:21:02.127 --> 00:21:04.529 align:middle line:84%
So by "culturally
appropriate," I

00:21:04.529 --> 00:21:06.932 align:middle line:84%
mean if you're
talking to someone

00:21:06.932 --> 00:21:09.334 align:middle line:84%
from a tropical climate, don't
make all of your sentences

00:21:09.334 --> 00:21:11.570 align:middle line:90%
about snow and ice.

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:14.006 align:middle line:84%
If you're talking to
someone from one religion,

00:21:14.006 --> 00:21:16.675 align:middle line:84%
don't make references to another
religion in your questions.

00:21:16.675 --> 00:21:18.777 align:middle line:84%
In fact, maybe try
to avoid references

00:21:18.777 --> 00:21:23.333 align:middle line:84%
to religion in your questions,
period-- basic stuff like that.

00:21:23.333 --> 00:21:25.083 align:middle line:84%
Like I say, I like to
start by asking them

00:21:25.083 --> 00:21:25.917 align:middle line:90%
their favorite food.

00:21:25.917 --> 00:21:30.589 align:middle line:84%
And then we ask lots of
questions about X!oo ate this

00:21:30.589 --> 00:21:33.458 align:middle line:84%
or X!oo is cooking that
and things like that.

00:21:33.458 --> 00:21:36.094 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, you make discoveries
about what counts as culturally

00:21:36.094 --> 00:21:36.595 align:middle line:90%
appropriate.

00:21:36.595 --> 00:21:40.198 align:middle line:84%
I once taught a Field Methods
class on a Semitic language

00:21:40.198 --> 00:21:42.701 align:middle line:90%
from Ethiopia called Chaha.

00:21:42.701 --> 00:21:43.535 align:middle line:90%
I started the class.

00:21:43.535 --> 00:21:45.837 align:middle line:84%
I was like, OK, I'm going
to get a basic sentence

00:21:45.837 --> 00:21:47.739 align:middle line:84%
with a subject, and
an object, and a verb.

00:21:47.739 --> 00:21:49.975 align:middle line:84%
How do you say, "The
man cooked the meat."

00:21:49.975 --> 00:21:51.476 align:middle line:84%
The guy said, "I
cannot say that."

00:21:51.476 --> 00:21:52.411 align:middle line:90%
I'm, like, OK, why?

00:21:52.411 --> 00:21:54.813 align:middle line:90%
He said, "Men do not cook.

00:21:54.813 --> 00:21:59.551 align:middle line:84%
And also, you do not
cook meat," he said.

00:21:59.551 --> 00:22:01.252 align:middle line:84%
"You're supposed
to eat meat raw,

00:22:01.252 --> 00:22:03.889 align:middle line:84%
unless there's something
wrong with it."

00:22:03.889 --> 00:22:04.423 align:middle line:90%
I said, "OK,

00:22:04.423 --> 00:22:06.768 align:middle line:84%
'The woman cooked the
cabbage.'" He's, like, "Yes,

00:22:06.768 --> 00:22:08.160 align:middle line:90%
we can say that."

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:13.231 align:middle line:90%
We started with that.

00:22:13.231 --> 00:22:18.337 align:middle line:84%
Whoops, I thought I told you
not to do that, computer.

00:22:18.337 --> 00:22:22.040 align:middle line:84%
And then, going up to
the first bullet point--

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:26.287 align:middle line:84%
I should work on this slide a
little more-- one of the things

00:22:26.287 --> 00:22:30.586 align:middle line:84%
you want to do, and this may
be hard, is to try to convince

00:22:30.586 --> 00:22:34.035 align:middle line:84%
the person you're working with
that you are really, really

00:22:34.035 --> 00:22:39.257 align:middle line:84%
interested in how
they actually talk.

00:22:39.257 --> 00:22:43.161 align:middle line:84%
Depending on the
language, a lot of people

00:22:43.161 --> 00:22:47.165 align:middle line:84%
are taught by the
educational systems

00:22:47.165 --> 00:22:50.235 align:middle line:84%
that they grew up with
that there is a correct way

00:22:50.235 --> 00:22:51.536 align:middle line:90%
to speak their language.

00:22:51.536 --> 00:22:54.005 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, they're taught
that their way of speaking it

00:22:54.005 --> 00:22:55.607 align:middle line:84%
is not the correct
one, that they're

00:22:55.607 --> 00:22:57.175 align:middle line:90%
bad at their native language.

00:22:57.175 --> 00:22:59.511 align:middle line:90%
That's distressingly common.

00:22:59.511 --> 00:23:01.113 align:middle line:84%
I do a fair amount
of work on Tagalog,

00:23:01.113 --> 00:23:03.166 align:middle line:84%
which is the language
from the Philippines.

00:23:03.166 --> 00:23:04.916 align:middle line:84%
And Tagalog speakers
often, when I'm first

00:23:04.916 --> 00:23:06.385 align:middle line:84%
starting to work
with them, they'll

00:23:06.385 --> 00:23:08.954 align:middle line:84%
start by telling me that they're
not very good at Tagalog,

00:23:08.954 --> 00:23:10.789 align:middle line:90%
which is their native language.

00:23:10.789 --> 00:23:12.624 align:middle line:90%
They grew up speaking Tagalog.

00:23:12.624 --> 00:23:14.493 align:middle line:84%
But what they mean
is they've been

00:23:14.493 --> 00:23:20.198 align:middle line:84%
taught that real Tagalog
doesn't have any borrowings

00:23:20.198 --> 00:23:22.868 align:middle line:84%
from Spanish or English,
that real Tagalog is

00:23:22.868 --> 00:23:25.971 align:middle line:84%
the Tagalog that's spoken
in isolated villages

00:23:25.971 --> 00:23:28.340 align:middle line:84%
that they've never been
to, where they speak

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:30.375 align:middle line:84%
the real, proper
kind of Tagalog,

00:23:30.375 --> 00:23:31.771 align:middle line:90%
which very few people speak.

00:23:31.771 --> 00:23:33.979 align:middle line:84%
And the people that I'm
working with don't speak that

00:23:33.979 --> 00:23:36.114 align:middle line:90%
and they feel bad about that.

00:23:36.114 --> 00:23:40.619 align:middle line:84%
So you have to make some attempt
to convince a person like that

00:23:40.619 --> 00:23:43.555 align:middle line:84%
that their version
of the language

00:23:43.555 --> 00:23:45.791 align:middle line:84%
is what you're
really interested in.

00:23:45.791 --> 00:23:49.628 align:middle line:84%
And maybe you can tell
them, I'm doing science.

00:23:49.628 --> 00:23:51.329 align:middle line:90%
I'm not here to judge you.

00:23:51.329 --> 00:23:53.732 align:middle line:84%
I'm not trying to find out
whether you speak correctly

00:23:53.732 --> 00:23:54.466 align:middle line:90%
or not.

00:23:54.466 --> 00:23:57.068 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to find
out how you speak.

00:23:57.068 --> 00:24:03.408 align:middle line:84%
And so I promise not to
send a note to your parents

00:24:03.408 --> 00:24:05.811 align:middle line:84%
or to your teachers in
high school reporting

00:24:05.811 --> 00:24:06.678 align:middle line:90%
on what you tell me.

00:24:06.678 --> 00:24:08.547 align:middle line:90%
That's not how this works.

00:24:08.547 --> 00:24:11.583 align:middle line:84%
This is I'm trying to understand
how people actually talk.

00:24:11.583 --> 00:24:13.852 align:middle line:84%
That's what
linguistics is about.

00:24:13.852 --> 00:24:17.155 align:middle line:84%
It's not about the rules that
you learn in high school,

00:24:17.155 --> 00:24:20.859 align:middle line:84%
which sometimes describe what
you actually do and sometimes

00:24:20.859 --> 00:24:22.994 align:middle line:90%
don't.

00:24:22.994 --> 00:24:24.095 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:24:24.095 --> 00:24:29.134 align:middle line:84%
These are all things
you may want to do.

00:24:29.134 --> 00:24:36.029 align:middle line:84%
All right, well, then,
today-- where are we-- today,

00:24:36.029 --> 00:24:39.044 align:middle line:84%
we're going to get
started on morphology.

00:24:39.044 --> 00:24:41.780 align:middle line:90%
That's our first topic.

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:44.583 align:middle line:84%
And so things that
we will get to,

00:24:44.583 --> 00:24:46.585 align:middle line:84%
depending on how far
we get today-- oh, yes.

00:24:46.585 --> 00:24:48.086 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Just as
a question, is it

00:24:48.086 --> 00:24:52.290 align:middle line:84%
appropriate to hypothesize about
paradigms or just one element

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:56.261 align:middle line:90%
of [INAUDIBLE]?

00:24:56.261 --> 00:24:58.730 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: I'm sorry,
can you say more about that?

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:00.632 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So if you're
trying to create,

00:25:00.632 --> 00:25:04.135 align:middle line:84%
for example, a
chart of pronouns,

00:25:04.135 --> 00:25:07.138 align:middle line:84%
is that a good thing
to do or should you

00:25:07.138 --> 00:25:08.473 align:middle line:90%
just focus on one word or what?

00:25:08.473 --> 00:25:09.741 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Oh, I see.

00:25:09.741 --> 00:25:13.111 align:middle line:84%
So in the problem set, we will
ask you to find out something

00:25:13.111 --> 00:25:14.679 align:middle line:90%
in particular.

00:25:14.679 --> 00:25:16.314 align:middle line:84%
So we'll ask you--
we'll have something

00:25:16.314 --> 00:25:17.883 align:middle line:90%
that we want you to find out.

00:25:17.883 --> 00:25:20.485 align:middle line:84%
So that would be the
thing to concentrate on.

00:25:20.485 --> 00:25:23.688 align:middle line:84%
In general, if you're
working on a language,

00:25:23.688 --> 00:25:26.658 align:middle line:84%
there kind of aren't guidelines
about what it's OK to work on

00:25:26.658 --> 00:25:27.592 align:middle line:90%
and what it's not.

00:25:27.592 --> 00:25:30.395 align:middle line:84%
So I'd encourage you, if
you're working with this person

00:25:30.395 --> 00:25:32.898 align:middle line:84%
and you discover that
you've answered the question

00:25:32.898 --> 00:25:35.800 align:middle line:84%
we've asked you to answer
quickly in the session,

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:38.837 align:middle line:84%
then one of the things you can
do-- part of being prepared--

00:25:38.837 --> 00:25:40.739 align:middle line:84%
is to do some thinking
about other things

00:25:40.739 --> 00:25:41.540 align:middle line:90%
you'd like to know.

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:43.842 align:middle line:84%
And that could be-- one
way is to sit down and get

00:25:43.842 --> 00:25:46.011 align:middle line:84%
a paradigm of
pronouns, or paradigm

00:25:46.011 --> 00:25:49.114 align:middle line:84%
of verbs in the present
tense, or whatever.

00:25:49.114 --> 00:25:52.335 align:middle line:84%
And of course, depending on
the language you're working on,

00:25:52.335 --> 00:25:54.085 align:middle line:84%
something like getting
a paradigm of verbs

00:25:54.085 --> 00:25:57.122 align:middle line:84%
in the present tense
could be trivial

00:25:57.122 --> 00:25:59.324 align:middle line:90%
or it could be impossible.

00:25:59.324 --> 00:26:01.793 align:middle line:84%
So you'll just have to find
out what kind of language

00:26:01.793 --> 00:26:03.695 align:middle line:90%
you're working on.

00:26:03.695 --> 00:26:05.397 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:26:05.397 --> 00:26:07.332 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So kind
of two questions.

00:26:07.332 --> 00:26:12.804 align:middle line:84%
One, when you say that you
want how people really speak,

00:26:12.804 --> 00:26:15.240 align:middle line:84%
if they're
incorporating a slang,

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:18.476 align:middle line:84%
is that something that you
want to just pick up or do you

00:26:18.476 --> 00:26:21.146 align:middle line:84%
want to kind of tell
them that [INAUDIBLE]??

00:26:21.146 --> 00:26:22.113 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Oh.

00:26:22.113 --> 00:26:25.450 align:middle line:84%
So basically, you want
to write down everything

00:26:25.450 --> 00:26:26.651 align:middle line:90%
that they tell you.

00:26:26.651 --> 00:26:29.888 align:middle line:84%
So if somebody says a
sentence, and then they say,

00:26:29.888 --> 00:26:32.424 align:middle line:90%
oh, but that's actually slang.

00:26:32.424 --> 00:26:35.093 align:middle line:84%
Here's the way you'd
say it without slang.

00:26:35.093 --> 00:26:37.228 align:middle line:84%
You want to write down
both of those sentences.

00:26:37.228 --> 00:26:38.630 align:middle line:90%
It's all data.

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:40.699 align:middle line:84%
You're going to try to
find everything else out.

00:26:40.699 --> 00:26:43.535 align:middle line:84%
It could be worth it
to try to find out,

00:26:43.535 --> 00:26:47.272 align:middle line:84%
when you say this as slang,
what do you mean exactly?

00:26:47.272 --> 00:26:53.211 align:middle line:84%
Because that could mean this
is only used by teenagers,

00:26:53.211 --> 00:26:55.513 align:middle line:84%
and everybody is expected
to grow out of this,

00:26:55.513 --> 00:26:59.084 align:middle line:84%
and my mother would wash my
mouth out with soap if she

00:26:59.084 --> 00:27:00.285 align:middle line:90%
heard me speaking this way.

00:27:00.285 --> 00:27:01.620 align:middle line:90%
It could be that.

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:03.655 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be-- so
in the Philippines,

00:27:03.655 --> 00:27:06.124 align:middle line:84%
I have people tell
me, oh, this is slang.

00:27:06.124 --> 00:27:09.494 align:middle line:84%
And what they mean is it's
not the Tagalog word for it.

00:27:09.494 --> 00:27:10.795 align:middle line:90%
It's the Spanish word for it.

00:27:10.795 --> 00:27:13.932 align:middle line:84%
And everyone uses the Spanish
word for it all the time.

00:27:13.932 --> 00:27:16.167 align:middle line:84%
It's just that we
feel guilty about it.

00:27:16.167 --> 00:27:18.870 align:middle line:84%
And so you'll have to find
out which of those situations

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:20.005 align:middle line:90%
you're in.

00:27:20.005 --> 00:27:23.108 align:middle line:84%
It would be surprising if you
could find it out on day one.

00:27:23.108 --> 00:27:25.343 align:middle line:84%
So for day one, probably
what you want to do

00:27:25.343 --> 00:27:27.545 align:middle line:84%
is write down both
versions of the sentence,

00:27:27.545 --> 00:27:30.749 align:middle line:84%
and maybe try to ask about what
they mean exactly when they

00:27:30.749 --> 00:27:31.383 align:middle line:90%
say it's slang.

00:27:31.383 --> 00:27:32.050 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:32.651 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:27:32.651 --> 00:27:34.085 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
So you may indeed

00:27:34.085 --> 00:27:36.221 align:middle line:84%
get someone who tells you,
here's how you say this.

00:27:36.221 --> 00:27:37.756 align:middle line:90%
Oh, but it's a bad way.

00:27:37.756 --> 00:27:41.559 align:middle line:84%
And you have to try to
find out what they mean.

00:27:41.559 --> 00:27:44.362 align:middle line:84%
But if they just
mean my high school

00:27:44.362 --> 00:27:45.864 align:middle line:84%
English teacher
would be displeased,

00:27:45.864 --> 00:27:47.999 align:middle line:84%
my high school whatever
teacher would be displeased

00:27:47.999 --> 00:27:51.169 align:middle line:84%
if they heard me say that,
then that's still data.

00:27:51.169 --> 00:27:55.540 align:middle line:84%
We're not passing judgment
on things like this.

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:57.375 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:27:57.375 --> 00:27:59.770 align:middle line:90%
And then the other question?

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:01.312 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I just have
another question

00:28:01.312 --> 00:28:09.454 align:middle line:84%
about when you said, like men
cooking, and how they like

00:28:09.454 --> 00:28:10.288 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:28:10.288 --> 00:28:12.490 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Can't say that, yeah.

00:28:12.490 --> 00:28:14.826 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I was wondering if
you were ever in a situation

00:28:14.826 --> 00:28:17.762 align:middle line:84%
where you just asked, oh,
well, hypothetically, how would

00:28:17.762 --> 00:28:19.164 align:middle line:90%
you say that or--

00:28:19.164 --> 00:28:21.666 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: So in that
particular situation, which

00:28:21.666 --> 00:28:24.202 align:middle line:84%
was day one of the
Field Methods class,

00:28:24.202 --> 00:28:26.838 align:middle line:84%
I decided not to have
that particular fight.

00:28:26.838 --> 00:28:31.142 align:middle line:84%
So we found out how to
use the verb "to cook."

00:28:31.142 --> 00:28:32.937 align:middle line:84%
And we found out
the word for "man."

00:28:32.937 --> 00:28:34.312 align:middle line:84%
And by the end,
we probably could

00:28:34.312 --> 00:28:36.614 align:middle line:84%
have figured out how to say,
"The man cooked the meat."

00:28:36.614 --> 00:28:42.721 align:middle line:84%
But that's another place
for not being judgmental

00:28:42.721 --> 00:28:45.356 align:middle line:84%
about the person you're
working with, I guess.

00:28:45.356 --> 00:28:47.726 align:middle line:84%
If that's what they
think, then fine.

00:28:47.726 --> 00:28:53.465 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to try
to talk them out of that.

00:28:53.465 --> 00:28:56.835 align:middle line:84%
We can, but not while
we're doing linguistics.

00:28:56.835 --> 00:28:58.103 align:middle line:90%
Other questions about that?

00:28:58.103 --> 00:29:00.939 align:middle line:90%
Those are good questions.

00:29:00.939 --> 00:29:02.340 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:08.546 align:middle line:84%
So morphology,
then-- so questions

00:29:08.546 --> 00:29:11.716 align:middle line:84%
like what do you know
when you know a word?

00:29:11.716 --> 00:29:12.884 align:middle line:90%
What's universal?

00:29:12.884 --> 00:29:13.985 align:middle line:90%
What's learned?

00:29:13.985 --> 00:29:17.455 align:middle line:84%
And why is the word
"unlockable" ambiguous?

00:29:17.455 --> 00:29:18.823 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about that, too.

00:29:18.823 --> 00:29:22.494 align:middle line:90%
So this is a cat.

00:29:22.494 --> 00:29:23.928 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:29:23.928 --> 00:29:25.029 align:middle line:90%
It's clear so far.

00:29:25.029 --> 00:29:29.834 align:middle line:84%
So the word "cat" refers
to certain kinds of things,

00:29:29.834 --> 00:29:33.838 align:middle line:84%
including the thing that
I've got a picture of here.

00:29:33.838 --> 00:29:39.210 align:middle line:84%
Now, I said, one of the things
we're going to talk about

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:42.447 align:middle line:84%
is what things about
language do you

00:29:42.447 --> 00:29:44.582 align:middle line:84%
have to learn when you
learn your native language,

00:29:44.582 --> 00:29:45.617 align:middle line:90%
or any language?

00:29:45.617 --> 00:29:48.520 align:middle line:84%
And what other
things are universal?

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:52.223 align:middle line:84%
So we talked a little bit
last time about the hypothesis

00:29:52.223 --> 00:29:55.293 align:middle line:84%
that our brains are
set up in such a way

00:29:55.293 --> 00:29:58.963 align:middle line:84%
that we can only create language
in some ways, and not others.

00:29:58.963 --> 00:30:01.366 align:middle line:84%
And one of the ways we find
out about those aspects

00:30:01.366 --> 00:30:05.127 align:middle line:84%
of our brains is by finding
things, surprising things, that

00:30:05.127 --> 00:30:08.711 align:middle line:84%
are true of every language that
we know anything about-- so

00:30:08.711 --> 00:30:10.742 align:middle line:84%
"universals," we call
those things, things

00:30:10.742 --> 00:30:12.544 align:middle line:90%
that are just always true.

00:30:12.544 --> 00:30:14.612 align:middle line:84%
Is it always true
in every language

00:30:14.612 --> 00:30:16.748 align:middle line:90%
that this is called a cat?

00:30:16.748 --> 00:30:20.151 align:middle line:84%
No, this is a lousy
candidate for a universal.

00:30:20.151 --> 00:30:24.055 align:middle line:84%
There are a zillion words for
cat out there in the world.

00:30:24.055 --> 00:30:26.124 align:middle line:90%
Here are some of them.

00:30:26.124 --> 00:30:31.462 align:middle line:84%
The famous linguist Saussure
who had a French phrase which

00:30:31.462 --> 00:30:33.832 align:middle line:84%
has been translated in
English as the arbitrariness

00:30:33.832 --> 00:30:37.769 align:middle line:84%
of the sign as the name
for this phenomenon.

00:30:37.769 --> 00:30:40.505 align:middle line:84%
The fact that this is
called a cat in English

00:30:40.505 --> 00:30:44.008 align:middle line:84%
doesn't follow from
anything else about English

00:30:44.008 --> 00:30:45.243 align:middle line:90%
or about any other language.

00:30:45.243 --> 00:30:47.512 align:middle line:84%
And if you're going to learn a
language, you've got to learn,

00:30:47.512 --> 00:30:49.280 align:middle line:84%
assuming that language
has a word for cat,

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:52.283 align:middle line:84%
you've got to learn that
language's word for cat.

00:30:52.283 --> 00:30:56.020 align:middle line:90%
Questions about that?

00:30:56.020 --> 00:31:01.259 align:middle line:84%
Arbitrariness of the sign--
quick sidebar about that--

00:31:01.259 --> 00:31:03.194 align:middle line:84%
of course, there
are places where

00:31:03.194 --> 00:31:04.963 align:middle line:84%
the arbitrariness
of the sign seems

00:31:04.963 --> 00:31:06.664 align:middle line:90%
not to be all that arbitrary.

00:31:06.664 --> 00:31:09.100 align:middle line:84%
So here are some
Passamaquoddy words.

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.369 align:middle line:84%
Passamaquoddy is a
Native American language,

00:31:11.369 --> 00:31:14.272 align:middle line:84%
Algonquian language
spoken up in Maine.

00:31:14.272 --> 00:31:16.441 align:middle line:84%
There's a kind of
bird in Passamaquoddy

00:31:16.441 --> 00:31:19.143 align:middle line:90%
which is called kuhkukhahs.

00:31:19.143 --> 00:31:25.650 align:middle line:84%
What kind of bird do you
think that is, kuhkukhahs?

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:26.784 align:middle line:90%
It could be a cuckoo.

00:31:26.784 --> 00:31:29.354 align:middle line:90%
Any other theories?

00:31:29.354 --> 00:31:30.355 align:middle line:90%
It's an owl.

00:31:30.355 --> 00:31:33.558 align:middle line:84%
So it's their name for
a great-horned owl.

00:31:33.558 --> 00:31:37.662 align:middle line:84%
They have another kind of
bird called a kocokikilahsis,

00:31:37.662 --> 00:31:39.364 align:middle line:90%
kocokikilahsis.

00:31:39.364 --> 00:31:39.898 align:middle line:90%
What's that?

00:31:39.898 --> 00:31:41.232 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:41.232 --> 00:31:43.635 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
No, it's a chickadee.

00:31:43.635 --> 00:31:46.237 align:middle line:90%
Chickadee, kocokikilahsis.

00:31:46.237 --> 00:31:48.740 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes, there
are things that

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:51.109 align:middle line:84%
are named after what
they sound like.

00:31:51.109 --> 00:31:56.547 align:middle line:84%
Japanese has a kind of
an adverb, "pikapika."

00:31:56.547 --> 00:32:00.818 align:middle line:84%
It refers to a kind of
light, a quality of light.

00:32:00.818 --> 00:32:02.654 align:middle line:84%
What quality of light
do you think it is?

00:32:02.654 --> 00:32:03.888 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Electric.

00:32:03.888 --> 00:32:06.925 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah, has
to do with electricity, yes.

00:32:06.925 --> 00:32:08.243 align:middle line:90%
Pikapika.

00:32:08.243 --> 00:32:08.993 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Flashing.

00:32:08.993 --> 00:32:09.494 align:middle line:90%
Flickering.

00:32:09.494 --> 00:32:11.462 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah,
flickering, flashing.

00:32:11.462 --> 00:32:11.996 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:32:11.996 --> 00:32:16.534 align:middle line:84%
So some of you may
have heard of Pikachu.

00:32:16.534 --> 00:32:18.836 align:middle line:84%
"Chu" is the noise
that a mouse makes.

00:32:18.836 --> 00:32:24.108 align:middle line:84%
It's like "squeak," so
Pikachu's like "flash squeak,"

00:32:24.108 --> 00:32:26.044 align:middle line:90%
that particular Pokemon.

00:32:26.044 --> 00:32:30.848 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, "pikapika" means
a flashing brilliant light.

00:32:30.848 --> 00:32:34.407 align:middle line:84%
If I had told you that
"pikapika" meant a soft,

00:32:34.407 --> 00:32:37.822 align:middle line:84%
gentle glow, that would have
been kind of surprising.

00:32:37.822 --> 00:32:40.124 align:middle line:84%
So there's something
non-arbitrary

00:32:40.124 --> 00:32:42.093 align:middle line:84%
about certain
places in the sign.

00:32:42.093 --> 00:32:43.695 align:middle line:84%
There are experiments
on this where

00:32:43.695 --> 00:32:46.564 align:middle line:90%
people show people shapes.

00:32:46.564 --> 00:32:50.668 align:middle line:84%
And you show people either a
jagged shape or a gentle shape

00:32:50.668 --> 00:32:52.036 align:middle line:90%
with lots of rounded corners.

00:32:52.036 --> 00:32:54.539 align:middle line:84%
And you say, "One of
these is a dub dub,

00:32:54.539 --> 00:32:56.074 align:middle line:90%
and the other is a tick tick."

00:32:56.074 --> 00:32:58.343 align:middle line:84%
And people are, like, "Yeah, the
thing with the jagged corners--

00:32:58.343 --> 00:32:59.260 align:middle line:90%
that's the tick tick."

00:32:59.260 --> 00:33:00.244 align:middle line:90%
That's clear.

00:33:00.244 --> 00:33:04.182 align:middle line:90%
I have that idea.

00:33:04.182 --> 00:33:07.852 align:middle line:84%
But putting aside those kinds
of cases, signs are arbitrary.

00:33:07.852 --> 00:33:09.487 align:middle line:90%
In English, we call cats "cats."

00:33:09.487 --> 00:33:11.789 align:middle line:84%
But in Japanese, they're
"neko" and they have

00:33:11.789 --> 00:33:15.827 align:middle line:90%
other names in other languages.

00:33:15.827 --> 00:33:18.329 align:middle line:84%
And even in cases
of onomatopoeia--

00:33:18.329 --> 00:33:21.165 align:middle line:84%
so these Passamaquoddy
words are onomatopoeic.

00:33:21.165 --> 00:33:24.369 align:middle line:84%
They are named for things that
are named after the sounds

00:33:24.369 --> 00:33:25.703 align:middle line:90%
that they make.

00:33:25.703 --> 00:33:29.674 align:middle line:90%
But take something like a frog.

00:33:29.674 --> 00:33:32.744 align:middle line:84%
They are onomatopoeic
words for what

00:33:32.744 --> 00:33:34.545 align:middle line:84%
frogs say in
different languages,

00:33:34.545 --> 00:33:36.848 align:middle line:84%
and they're pretty strikingly
different from language

00:33:36.848 --> 00:33:37.348 align:middle line:90%
to language.

00:33:37.348 --> 00:33:39.598 align:middle line:84%
Everybody seems to be able
to hear something different

00:33:39.598 --> 00:33:43.988 align:middle line:90%
in the call of a frog.

00:33:43.988 --> 00:33:48.659 align:middle line:84%
So if we're trying to
make a list of everything

00:33:48.659 --> 00:33:51.662 align:middle line:84%
that you know about
your native language,

00:33:51.662 --> 00:33:53.197 align:middle line:84%
if you're talking
about English, one

00:33:53.197 --> 00:33:54.699 align:middle line:84%
of the things that
we've got to list

00:33:54.699 --> 00:33:58.803 align:middle line:84%
is that you know the
word "cat" means cat.

00:33:58.803 --> 00:34:01.472 align:middle line:84%
And that's one of the
many bits of information

00:34:01.472 --> 00:34:02.373 align:middle line:90%
that's in your head.

00:34:02.373 --> 00:34:03.841 align:middle line:90%
Doesn't follow from anything.

00:34:03.841 --> 00:34:07.245 align:middle line:90%
We've just got to learn that.

00:34:07.245 --> 00:34:10.181 align:middle line:84%
So now, let's think what else
is in your mental lexicon,

00:34:10.181 --> 00:34:15.753 align:middle line:84%
your little list of everything
you know about your language?

00:34:15.753 --> 00:34:19.824 align:middle line:84%
Well, this could be lexical
entry number two, "cats."

00:34:19.824 --> 00:34:20.891 align:middle line:90%
Maybe.

00:34:20.891 --> 00:34:22.994 align:middle line:84%
So you could have a
lexical entry for "cat"

00:34:22.994 --> 00:34:25.663 align:middle line:84%
that says cat refers to these
little, furry things that

00:34:25.663 --> 00:34:26.697 align:middle line:90%
chase mice.

00:34:26.697 --> 00:34:28.666 align:middle line:84%
And then you could
have another entry,

00:34:28.666 --> 00:34:32.603 align:middle line:84%
"cats," that refers to more
than one of those things.

00:34:32.603 --> 00:34:37.708 align:middle line:84%
That's one way we could
do our lexical entries.

00:34:37.708 --> 00:34:42.980 align:middle line:84%
But that seems kind of, I don't
know, wasteful and inelegant.

00:34:42.980 --> 00:34:47.784 align:middle line:84%
Because what we're
failing to recognize,

00:34:47.784 --> 00:34:50.588 align:middle line:84%
if we decide to have one
lexical entry for "cat"

00:34:50.588 --> 00:34:53.658 align:middle line:84%
and another one for "cats," is
that those two lexical entries

00:34:53.658 --> 00:34:55.025 align:middle line:90%
seem to have a lot in common.

00:34:55.025 --> 00:34:57.094 align:middle line:90%
They both start with "cat."

00:34:57.094 --> 00:35:01.023 align:middle line:84%
And they both refer to groups
of things-- one of them

00:35:01.023 --> 00:35:03.701 align:middle line:84%
just one of those things and
the other one more than one

00:35:03.701 --> 00:35:06.704 align:middle line:84%
of those things-- that
are furry, and chase mice,

00:35:06.704 --> 00:35:10.875 align:middle line:84%
and chase laser
pointers, those things.

00:35:10.875 --> 00:35:14.879 align:middle line:84%
And moreover, the
difference between them

00:35:14.879 --> 00:35:16.948 align:middle line:84%
has to do with this
S that's at the end.

00:35:16.948 --> 00:35:21.052 align:middle line:84%
And we see pairs of words that
have that S at the end in lots

00:35:21.052 --> 00:35:21.838 align:middle line:90%
of places.

00:35:21.838 --> 00:35:24.088 align:middle line:84%
It's not just "cat" and
"cats," it's "dog" and "dogs,"

00:35:24.088 --> 00:35:26.546 align:middle line:84%
and "banana" and "bananas,"
and "computer" and "computers."

00:35:26.546 --> 00:35:29.627 align:middle line:84%
There's lots of
things like that.

00:35:29.627 --> 00:35:32.896 align:middle line:84%
So there are two kinds of
lexicons that we could imagine

00:35:32.896 --> 00:35:36.860 align:middle line:84%
having, then-- one where we
have lexical entries for "cat"

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:39.036 align:middle line:84%
and "cats," and "dog" and
"dogs," and "computer"

00:35:39.036 --> 00:35:42.831 align:middle line:84%
and "computers," and so on, and
another where we have lexical

00:35:42.831 --> 00:35:47.544 align:middle line:84%
entries for "cat," and "dog,"
and "banana," and "computer,"

00:35:47.544 --> 00:35:51.994 align:middle line:84%
and "-s"-- a lexical entry for
this other thing that you get

00:35:51.994 --> 00:35:55.586 align:middle line:84%
to add to nouns to
make them plural.

00:35:55.586 --> 00:36:00.258 align:middle line:84%
And for one thing, if
there's any premium

00:36:00.258 --> 00:36:02.493 align:middle line:84%
at all on storage
space, if it's at all

00:36:02.493 --> 00:36:05.429 align:middle line:84%
useful to have a lexicon
that's not all that large,

00:36:05.429 --> 00:36:07.365 align:middle line:84%
well, lexicon
number one is going

00:36:07.365 --> 00:36:09.467 align:middle line:84%
to be larger than
lexicon number two

00:36:09.467 --> 00:36:13.771 align:middle line:84%
as long as you have any
nouns in your lexicon at all.

00:36:13.771 --> 00:36:16.140 align:middle line:84%
Because lexicon number
one-- if you have n nouns,

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:19.944 align:middle line:84%
you need 2n forms, singular
and plurals, for each of them.

00:36:19.944 --> 00:36:23.214 align:middle line:84%
Whereas lexicon number
two, if you have n nouns,

00:36:23.214 --> 00:36:28.853 align:middle line:84%
you need n plus 1 forms-- forms
for all your nouns plus "-s."

00:36:28.853 --> 00:36:30.488 align:middle line:90%
I'm oversimplifying, obviously.

00:36:30.488 --> 00:36:34.125 align:middle line:84%
Not every noun makes its plural
with an S. Talk about that.

00:36:34.125 --> 00:36:37.161 align:middle line:84%
But do people see
what I mean there?

00:36:37.161 --> 00:36:39.030 align:middle line:84%
So there's at least
a prima facie reason

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:44.502 align:middle line:84%
to take seriously the idea that
a word like "cats" is complex.

00:36:44.502 --> 00:36:45.970 align:middle line:90%
It has two parts.

00:36:45.970 --> 00:36:51.842 align:middle line:84%
There's the "cat" part
and there's the "-s" part.

00:36:51.842 --> 00:36:55.846 align:middle line:90%
Anyone want to object to that?

00:36:55.846 --> 00:36:57.148 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:36:57.148 --> 00:36:59.717 align:middle line:84%
In some languages,
it would be really

00:36:59.717 --> 00:37:02.286 align:middle line:84%
wasteful to try to
have a lexical entry

00:37:02.286 --> 00:37:04.155 align:middle line:90%
for every form of every word.

00:37:04.155 --> 00:37:06.090 align:middle line:84%
Here's a language
of Papua New Guinea.

00:37:06.090 --> 00:37:08.125 align:middle line:90%
It's called Nimboran.

00:37:08.125 --> 00:37:11.009 align:middle line:84%
It has four different
tenses-- future,

00:37:11.009 --> 00:37:14.065 align:middle line:84%
and past, and recent
past, and distant past.

00:37:14.065 --> 00:37:16.567 align:middle line:84%
There are suffixes
on the verb that

00:37:16.567 --> 00:37:19.503 align:middle line:84%
tell you things about the
subject and the object,

00:37:19.503 --> 00:37:22.740 align:middle line:84%
where there are 14 different
types of subjects and objects

00:37:22.740 --> 00:37:23.941 align:middle line:90%
that it recognizes.

00:37:23.941 --> 00:37:28.946 align:middle line:84%
So the subject could be
singular, or dual, or plural.

00:37:28.946 --> 00:37:30.281 align:middle line:90%
There are locative suffixes.

00:37:30.281 --> 00:37:31.549 align:middle line:90%
There are aspects.

00:37:31.549 --> 00:37:33.851 align:middle line:84%
The result is that if you
have a transitive verb,

00:37:33.851 --> 00:37:37.888 align:middle line:84%
it has 23 and 1/2
thousand forms.

00:37:37.888 --> 00:37:39.824 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if you have
a transitive verb

00:37:39.824 --> 00:37:42.293 align:middle line:84%
and you are just going to
list all of these suffixes,

00:37:42.293 --> 00:37:46.197 align:middle line:84%
well, you need 49
of the suffixes.

00:37:46.197 --> 00:37:49.810 align:middle line:84%
So I said for English,
if you were trying

00:37:49.810 --> 00:37:52.826 align:middle line:84%
to list every form-- "cat,"
"cats," "dog," "dogs,"

00:37:52.826 --> 00:37:54.805 align:middle line:84%
"computer," "computers,"
you would need

00:37:54.805 --> 00:37:57.608 align:middle line:90%
2n forms if you have n nouns.

00:37:57.608 --> 00:38:00.911 align:middle line:84%
Well, in Nimboran,
if you have v verbs,

00:38:00.911 --> 00:38:05.716 align:middle line:84%
you need 23,520v lexical entries
if you're going to list every

00:38:05.716 --> 00:38:07.285 align:middle line:90%
lexical entry.

00:38:07.285 --> 00:38:09.654 align:middle line:90%
Take pity on the Nimborans.

00:38:09.654 --> 00:38:14.692 align:middle line:84%
Don't make them lists
23,520 forms in their heads.

00:38:14.692 --> 00:38:18.863 align:middle line:84%
Allow them, instead, to list
their verbs, and the suffixes

00:38:18.863 --> 00:38:21.124 align:middle line:84%
that go on the verbs,
and rules for how

00:38:21.124 --> 00:38:22.166 align:middle line:90%
those things go together.

00:38:22.166 --> 00:38:24.101 align:middle line:84%
That will be computationally
much simpler.

00:38:24.101 --> 00:38:27.171 align:middle line:84%
They will not have to
store as much stuff.

00:38:27.171 --> 00:38:29.440 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:32.543 align:middle line:84%
So that's a hypothesis that
we're going to take seriously

00:38:32.543 --> 00:38:38.482 align:middle line:84%
that our mental lexicon-- yeah,
it contains words like "cat,"

00:38:38.482 --> 00:38:42.720 align:middle line:84%
but it also contains
parts of words like "-s,"

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:47.992 align:middle line:84%
this suffix that you can add
to "cat" to make it plural.

00:38:47.992 --> 00:38:51.329 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going
to try to understand

00:38:51.329 --> 00:38:57.168 align:middle line:84%
how those kinds of
things interact now.

00:38:57.168 --> 00:39:01.717 align:middle line:84%
We actually have various kinds
of evidence-- it's not just,

00:39:01.717 --> 00:39:04.508 align:middle line:84%
well, it would save
space in your lexicon--

00:39:04.508 --> 00:39:09.880 align:middle line:84%
Various kinds of evidence that
human beings do divide words

00:39:09.880 --> 00:39:11.982 align:middle line:84%
into these parts, that
a word like "cats"

00:39:11.982 --> 00:39:15.553 align:middle line:84%
is a word with two
parts, "cat" and "-s."

00:39:15.553 --> 00:39:19.824 align:middle line:84%
One straightforward one is the
fact that these are productive.

00:39:19.824 --> 00:39:26.152 align:middle line:84%
So if I make up a new word
in English, if I tell you

00:39:26.152 --> 00:39:30.020 align:middle line:84%
I have invented a cool,
new machine which everyone

00:39:30.020 --> 00:39:33.204 align:middle line:84%
is going to want to
have in their house--

00:39:33.204 --> 00:39:36.486 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be extremely
useful-- and it is called,

00:39:36.486 --> 00:39:38.376 align:middle line:90%
let's see, I'll give it a name.

00:39:38.376 --> 00:39:41.512 align:middle line:90%
It's called a Blurk.

00:39:41.512 --> 00:39:42.947 align:middle line:90%
So that's my brand name.

00:39:42.947 --> 00:39:45.316 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to sell the Blurk.

00:39:45.316 --> 00:39:46.317 align:middle line:90%
I'll make millions.

00:39:46.317 --> 00:39:49.887 align:middle line:84%
Don't worry, I'll remember all
of you when I'm on my yacht,

00:39:49.887 --> 00:39:52.089 align:middle line:90%
working on my tan.

00:39:52.089 --> 00:39:59.130 align:middle line:84%
And the Blurk-- what's the
plural of "Blurk" going to be?

00:39:59.130 --> 00:39:59.990 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: "Blurks."

00:39:59.990 --> 00:40:01.532 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Yeah, if you want

00:40:01.532 --> 00:40:05.202 align:middle line:84%
to buy more than one Blurk,
as I recommend that you do,

00:40:05.202 --> 00:40:08.606 align:middle line:84%
those things are
going to be Blurks.

00:40:08.606 --> 00:40:11.242 align:middle line:84%
And there's no
difficulty about that.

00:40:11.242 --> 00:40:14.645 align:middle line:84%
You know that English has this
suffix that makes plurals,

00:40:14.645 --> 00:40:17.948 align:middle line:84%
and you can add it to a word
that you've never heard before

00:40:17.948 --> 00:40:20.117 align:middle line:90%
because I just made it up.

00:40:20.117 --> 00:40:22.988 align:middle line:84%
That would be surprising if your
mental lexicon contained "cat,"

00:40:22.988 --> 00:40:24.988 align:middle line:84%
"cats," "dog," "dogs,"
"computer," "computers"--

00:40:24.988 --> 00:40:28.554 align:middle line:84%
just a bunch of words that
accidentally kind of resemble

00:40:28.554 --> 00:40:29.326 align:middle line:90%
each other.

00:40:29.326 --> 00:40:31.829 align:middle line:84%
It looks as though that's not
what your lexicon contains.

00:40:31.829 --> 00:40:34.899 align:middle line:84%
It contains a bunch of nouns
and a general principle

00:40:34.899 --> 00:40:36.567 align:middle line:84%
for how you make
things plural, which

00:40:36.567 --> 00:40:38.969 align:middle line:90%
you get to apply to "Blurk."

00:40:38.969 --> 00:40:41.839 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:40:41.839 --> 00:40:44.308 align:middle line:84%
It's been shown-- we've
shown this classic series

00:40:44.308 --> 00:40:49.246 align:middle line:84%
of experiments by
Berko in the '50s.

00:40:49.246 --> 00:40:52.183 align:middle line:84%
It's been shown that very
young children can do this.

00:40:52.183 --> 00:40:53.918 align:middle line:84%
We will talk more
about this later,

00:40:53.918 --> 00:40:56.053 align:middle line:90%
but she had these pictures.

00:40:56.053 --> 00:40:57.988 align:middle line:84%
So she would show kids
a picture of something.

00:40:57.988 --> 00:40:59.590 align:middle line:84%
She'd say, "This
is a wug," and then

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:01.843 align:middle line:84%
the picture of two of the
things that she had created.

00:41:01.843 --> 00:41:03.093 align:middle line:90%
And now there are two of them.

00:41:03.093 --> 00:41:07.064 align:middle line:84%
There are two-- and the
kids would all go, "Wugs."

00:41:07.064 --> 00:41:11.902 align:middle line:84%
Later, people began selling
things with wugs on them.

00:41:11.902 --> 00:41:14.238 align:middle line:84%
They're a big deal
among linguists.

00:41:14.238 --> 00:41:19.009 align:middle line:84%
So one reason we have to think
that you have lexical entry

00:41:19.009 --> 00:41:22.259 align:middle line:84%
for "-s" is that it's a
procedure that you can apply

00:41:22.259 --> 00:41:23.881 align:middle line:90%
to novel words.

00:41:23.881 --> 00:41:26.650 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, we seem to
be hungry to create

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:29.820 align:middle line:90%
these things, these suffixes.

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:31.822 align:middle line:90%
Classic case is "gate."

00:41:31.822 --> 00:41:35.659 align:middle line:84%
So the original
gate was Watergate,

00:41:35.659 --> 00:41:41.432 align:middle line:84%
which was before I was born,
so a really long time ago.

00:41:41.432 --> 00:41:43.234 align:middle line:84%
So sometime in the
previous millennium,

00:41:43.234 --> 00:41:48.405 align:middle line:84%
there was this scandal involving
President Nixon and something

00:41:48.405 --> 00:41:51.742 align:middle line:84%
that happened at
the Watergate Hotel.

00:41:51.742 --> 00:41:55.746 align:middle line:84%
The Watergate scandal was
not a scandal about water.

00:41:55.746 --> 00:41:58.048 align:middle line:84%
It got its name because it
happened at the Watergate

00:41:58.048 --> 00:41:58.883 align:middle line:90%
Hotel.

00:41:58.883 --> 00:42:02.920 align:middle line:84%
But people took the
"gate" of Watergate--

00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:06.423 align:middle line:84%
because Watergate is so easily
divisible into these two parts,

00:42:06.423 --> 00:42:09.448 align:middle line:84%
because "water" is a
word-- people took "gate,"

00:42:09.448 --> 00:42:11.362 align:middle line:90%
and it's become a suffix now.

00:42:11.362 --> 00:42:15.232 align:middle line:84%
So it's attached to words
to give names for scandals.

00:42:15.232 --> 00:42:21.105 align:middle line:84%
So Nixon had Watergate,
Clinton had Monicagate.

00:42:21.105 --> 00:42:21.906 align:middle line:90%
There was Irangate.

00:42:21.906 --> 00:42:23.908 align:middle line:90%
There have been various gates.

00:42:23.908 --> 00:42:27.244 align:middle line:84%
So "-gate" is now a
suffix meaning "scandal."

00:42:27.244 --> 00:42:29.206 align:middle line:84%
People seem to be hungry
to create suffixes,

00:42:29.206 --> 00:42:30.748 align:middle line:84%
even where they
weren't there before.

00:42:30.748 --> 00:42:31.325 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:42:31.325 --> 00:42:33.117 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So like
"-burger" and "-mageddon"?

00:42:33.117 --> 00:42:35.742 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yes, right,
like "-mageddon" or "-palooza."

00:42:35.742 --> 00:42:39.256 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there are many of
these that people create out

00:42:39.256 --> 00:42:40.057 align:middle line:90%
of whole cloth.

00:42:40.057 --> 00:42:43.761 align:middle line:90%
That's a very good example.

00:42:43.761 --> 00:42:46.564 align:middle line:84%
Other examples in the
history of English--

00:42:46.564 --> 00:42:50.690 align:middle line:84%
there are words like
"sculptor," and "beggar,"

00:42:50.690 --> 00:42:55.773 align:middle line:84%
and "swindler," which entered
the languages as nouns.

00:42:55.773 --> 00:42:58.976 align:middle line:84%
So "sculptor" is
from a Latin verb.

00:42:58.976 --> 00:43:01.635 align:middle line:84%
It involves a Latin
suffix "-tor,"

00:43:01.635 --> 00:43:06.717 align:middle line:84%
which is used to create
people who do things.

00:43:06.717 --> 00:43:09.753 align:middle line:84%
And "beggar" and
"swindler," similarly.

00:43:09.753 --> 00:43:13.057 align:middle line:84%
It's an accident that
they end in "-er."

00:43:13.057 --> 00:43:18.455 align:middle line:84%
But "-er" in English is a suffix
that you add to verbs to create

00:43:18.455 --> 00:43:20.731 align:middle line:84%
nouns meaning person
who does that.

00:43:20.731 --> 00:43:23.634 align:middle line:84%
So you get "teach" and "teacher"
or "sing" and "singer."

00:43:23.634 --> 00:43:26.870 align:middle line:84%
And so through a process of
what's called back formation,

00:43:26.870 --> 00:43:29.440 align:middle line:84%
people heard these words
that ended in "-er,"

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:31.308 align:middle line:84%
and they thought,
"Oh, that's '-er.'

00:43:31.308 --> 00:43:32.843 align:middle line:84%
I know that from
'teach, teacher.'"

00:43:32.843 --> 00:43:36.246 align:middle line:84%
And so they made up verbs
that didn't exist before,

00:43:36.246 --> 00:43:40.784 align:middle line:84%
like "sculpt," and "beg,"
and swindle by "removing"

00:43:40.784 --> 00:43:44.989 align:middle line:84%
the "-er," basically, which
hadn't originally been added.

00:43:44.989 --> 00:43:46.624 align:middle line:90%
Similar thing with "pea."

00:43:46.624 --> 00:43:51.362 align:middle line:84%
So originally, "pease"
was a mass noun.

00:43:51.362 --> 00:43:57.635 align:middle line:84%
It referred to this group of
small, little, round, green

00:43:57.635 --> 00:43:59.403 align:middle line:84%
things that you
could eat lots of.

00:43:59.403 --> 00:44:00.938 align:middle line:90%
So "pease" was a mass noun.

00:44:00.938 --> 00:44:03.841 align:middle line:84%
It was like "water," or
"sand," or "ketchup."

00:44:03.841 --> 00:44:08.379 align:middle line:84%
But it happened to end in "-s,"
so it looks like a plural.

00:44:08.379 --> 00:44:10.114 align:middle line:84%
And it refers to
something that you

00:44:10.114 --> 00:44:13.017 align:middle line:84%
can divide into lots of-- there
are the individual little,

00:44:13.017 --> 00:44:14.251 align:middle line:90%
round, green things.

00:44:14.251 --> 00:44:17.021 align:middle line:84%
And so people made
up a singular "pea."

00:44:17.021 --> 00:44:18.355 align:middle line:90%
And that's where that came from.

00:44:18.355 --> 00:44:21.330 align:middle line:84%
But originally, the word
that we get from old English

00:44:21.330 --> 00:44:21.925 align:middle line:90%
is "pease."

00:44:21.925 --> 00:44:25.963 align:middle line:84%
And it was originally
not a plural.

00:44:25.963 --> 00:44:28.565 align:middle line:84%
People reinterpreted
it as a plural.

00:44:28.565 --> 00:44:33.537 align:middle line:84%
So our mental
dictionary, our lexicon,

00:44:33.537 --> 00:44:36.306 align:middle line:84%
seems to have these things,
these parts of words.

00:44:36.306 --> 00:44:40.744 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, so we can use
them on unfamiliar words.

00:44:40.744 --> 00:44:44.014 align:middle line:84%
And we seem to be hungry
to create them in places

00:44:44.014 --> 00:44:47.184 align:middle line:84%
where there's any
suggestion that a word could

00:44:47.184 --> 00:44:49.353 align:middle line:90%
be divided into parts.

00:44:49.353 --> 00:44:52.149 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:52.149 --> 00:44:53.857 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: When you use
the word "lexicon,"

00:44:53.857 --> 00:44:56.960 align:middle line:84%
do we think of it as
a dictionary of words

00:44:56.960 --> 00:44:57.528 align:middle line:90%
and workings?

00:44:57.528 --> 00:45:00.230 align:middle line:84%
Or are we talking about
the rules as well?

00:45:00.230 --> 00:45:02.533 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
So it's day two.

00:45:02.533 --> 00:45:05.202 align:middle line:84%
It's not too clear yet what
we're talking about, is it?

00:45:05.202 --> 00:45:07.037 align:middle line:84%
But I think right
now, all we have

00:45:07.037 --> 00:45:10.059 align:middle line:84%
to be talking about is a list
of the words and the morphemes.

00:45:10.059 --> 00:45:12.309 align:middle line:84%
And you're absolutely right--
another thing that we'll

00:45:12.309 --> 00:45:15.179 align:middle line:84%
have to have is a set of rules
for how those things combine.

00:45:15.179 --> 00:45:17.881 align:middle line:84%
Actually, that's what we're
going to move on to next.

00:45:17.881 --> 00:45:20.884 align:middle line:90%
That's absolutely right.

00:45:20.884 --> 00:45:23.287 align:middle line:84%
So for example, on day one,
we talked about the fact

00:45:23.287 --> 00:45:25.889 align:middle line:84%
that the S has different
pronunciations depending

00:45:25.889 --> 00:45:27.491 align:middle line:90%
on what you're attaching it to.

00:45:27.491 --> 00:45:30.794 align:middle line:84%
We want to have an account
of that kind of thing.

00:45:30.794 --> 00:45:33.764 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes it sounds like an
S and sometimes like a Z.

00:45:33.764 --> 00:45:36.467 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, there are nouns
that don't take an S at all.

00:45:36.467 --> 00:45:38.736 align:middle line:90%
So the plural of "ox" is "oxen."

00:45:38.736 --> 00:45:40.170 align:middle line:84%
I'll have to talk
about that, too.

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:41.739 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:45:41.739 --> 00:45:44.308 align:middle line:90%
Other questions about this?

00:45:44.308 --> 00:45:47.311 align:middle line:84%
These are all reasons to make
you take seriously the idea

00:45:47.311 --> 00:45:49.747 align:middle line:84%
that words can be
divided into parts,

00:45:49.747 --> 00:45:53.517 align:middle line:84%
that "cats" consists of "cat"
plus a suffix, both of which

00:45:53.517 --> 00:45:56.053 align:middle line:84%
are maybe listed in
the set of things

00:45:56.053 --> 00:45:59.289 align:middle line:84%
that you know in the
lexicon, this list of things

00:45:59.289 --> 00:46:03.961 align:middle line:90%
you know about your language.

00:46:03.961 --> 00:46:07.231 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:46:07.231 --> 00:46:09.133 align:middle line:84%
So now, some
terminology, including

00:46:09.133 --> 00:46:15.372 align:middle line:84%
a word that Raquel just said,
so get all of you caught up.

00:46:15.372 --> 00:46:18.842 align:middle line:84%
Words like "cats," or "dogs,"
or "atrocity," or "culpable,"

00:46:18.842 --> 00:46:21.945 align:middle line:84%
or "unworthy"-- we're
saying these are

00:46:21.945 --> 00:46:23.781 align:middle line:90%
words that have multiple parts.

00:46:23.781 --> 00:46:25.949 align:middle line:84%
We can productively
divide them and it's

00:46:25.949 --> 00:46:29.086 align:middle line:84%
useful to divide
them into parts.

00:46:29.086 --> 00:46:32.256 align:middle line:84%
Any part of a word,
these parts of words,

00:46:32.256 --> 00:46:34.024 align:middle line:90%
are what are called morphemes.

00:46:34.024 --> 00:46:35.692 align:middle line:90%
So "cat" is a morpheme.

00:46:35.692 --> 00:46:36.894 align:middle line:90%
"Dog" is a morpheme.

00:46:36.894 --> 00:46:39.029 align:middle line:90%
"-s" is a morphine.

00:46:39.029 --> 00:46:42.833 align:middle line:84%
So is "atroc-" and
"-ity" in "atrocity."

00:46:42.833 --> 00:46:44.067 align:middle line:90%
Those are all morphemes.

00:46:44.067 --> 00:46:49.940 align:middle line:84%
So that's the name for these
parts of words combined.

00:46:49.940 --> 00:46:53.610 align:middle line:84%
People sometimes
distinguish the root,

00:46:53.610 --> 00:46:59.249 align:middle line:84%
which is the thing to which
prefixes or suffixes are added,

00:46:59.249 --> 00:47:03.590 align:middle line:84%
things like "cat," and "dog,"
and "culp-," and "worth-,"

00:47:03.590 --> 00:47:04.555 align:middle line:90%
and "atroc-."

00:47:04.555 --> 00:47:07.468 align:middle line:90%
Those are all roots.

00:47:07.468 --> 00:47:08.926 align:middle line:84%
So they are the
things to which you

00:47:08.926 --> 00:47:11.995 align:middle line:84%
are going to add
suffixes or prefixes,

00:47:11.995 --> 00:47:14.031 align:middle line:90%
other kinds of things.

00:47:14.031 --> 00:47:18.165 align:middle line:84%
And then "-s" and "-ity"
and "-able" and "un-,"

00:47:18.165 --> 00:47:20.003 align:middle line:90%
those are all affixes.

00:47:20.003 --> 00:47:22.272 align:middle line:84%
These are all-- most
of them are suffixes.

00:47:22.272 --> 00:47:23.707 align:middle line:90%
One of them is a prefix.

00:47:23.707 --> 00:47:29.179 align:middle line:84%
But "affix" is a general
term for these things

00:47:29.179 --> 00:47:33.843 align:middle line:90%
that you add to roots.

00:47:33.843 --> 00:47:35.385 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
one last distinction

00:47:35.385 --> 00:47:38.422 align:middle line:84%
to draw, which is between
things that are free

00:47:38.422 --> 00:47:40.123 align:middle line:90%
and things that are bound.

00:47:40.123 --> 00:47:43.594 align:middle line:84%
So to say that a
morpheme is free

00:47:43.594 --> 00:47:45.829 align:middle line:84%
is to say that you don't
have to add anything to it.

00:47:45.829 --> 00:47:47.464 align:middle line:90%
It can be a word by itself.

00:47:47.464 --> 00:47:50.667 align:middle line:90%
So "cat" is a free morpheme.

00:47:50.667 --> 00:47:53.503 align:middle line:90%
"-s" is a bound morpheme.

00:47:53.503 --> 00:47:57.341 align:middle line:90%
So is "culp-" and "atroc-."

00:47:57.341 --> 00:48:01.545 align:middle line:84%
So "culp" isn't a word,
"culpable" is a word.

00:48:01.545 --> 00:48:06.416 align:middle line:84%
"culp-" is a root,
so it's a bound root.

00:48:06.416 --> 00:48:08.652 align:middle line:84%
You need to add a suffix
to it, but it's something

00:48:08.652 --> 00:48:10.787 align:middle line:90%
to which you add suffixes.

00:48:10.787 --> 00:48:11.555 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:48:11.555 --> 00:48:14.224 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Are all three
morphemes in English?

00:48:14.224 --> 00:48:20.597 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yes.

00:48:20.597 --> 00:48:21.899 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good question.

00:48:21.899 --> 00:48:22.733 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:48:22.733 --> 00:48:34.229 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: That doesn't
necessarily apply [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:34.229 --> 00:48:35.812 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Yeah, so clitics--

00:48:35.812 --> 00:48:37.514 align:middle line:84%
I wonder whether we
will manage to talk

00:48:37.514 --> 00:48:38.515 align:middle line:90%
about clitics seriously.

00:48:38.515 --> 00:48:39.449 align:middle line:90%
But yes, you're right.

00:48:39.449 --> 00:48:41.685 align:middle line:84%
There's another kind
of thing-- "clitic"

00:48:41.685 --> 00:48:44.700 align:middle line:84%
is this name for these things--
that needs to be attached

00:48:44.700 --> 00:48:48.392 align:middle line:90%
to something else.

00:48:48.392 --> 00:48:52.829 align:middle line:90%
Yes, that's a very good point.

00:48:52.829 --> 00:48:55.332 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:48:55.332 --> 00:48:56.767 align:middle line:90%
So this is just all terminology.

00:48:56.767 --> 00:49:00.003 align:middle line:84%
It's terminology you will
hear me use, probably.

00:49:00.003 --> 00:49:03.340 align:middle line:84%
A word can consist of just one
morpheme, like "cat" or "dog."

00:49:03.340 --> 00:49:06.223 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
other words, like "cats"

00:49:06.223 --> 00:49:13.850 align:middle line:84%
or "industrialization," that
consist of multiple morphemes.

00:49:13.850 --> 00:49:16.520 align:middle line:84%
And then one other
distinction that people draw--

00:49:16.520 --> 00:49:19.957 align:middle line:84%
and I hope that nobody will
ask me to define this one very

00:49:19.957 --> 00:49:23.694 align:middle line:84%
carefully, because it's a quick
and dirty distinction people

00:49:23.694 --> 00:49:24.194 align:middle line:90%
draw.

00:49:24.194 --> 00:49:29.166 align:middle line:84%
I promise that I
will never harm you

00:49:29.166 --> 00:49:31.902 align:middle line:90%
in any way for misusing this.

00:49:31.902 --> 00:49:35.005 align:middle line:84%
So you will not get counted
off on problem sets for this.

00:49:35.005 --> 00:49:37.174 align:middle line:84%
People sometimes draw a
distinction between what are

00:49:37.174 --> 00:49:41.011 align:middle line:84%
called "open-class morphemes"
and "closed-class morphemes."

00:49:41.011 --> 00:49:43.347 align:middle line:84%
The idea is open-class
morphemes are

00:49:43.347 --> 00:49:45.949 align:middle line:84%
supposed to be
morphemes for which

00:49:45.949 --> 00:49:48.385 align:middle line:90%
you could make up another one.

00:49:48.385 --> 00:49:51.722 align:middle line:84%
You could add to the class of
open-class morphemes, that's

00:49:51.722 --> 00:49:52.823 align:middle line:90%
why it's called open.

00:49:52.823 --> 00:49:59.196 align:middle line:84%
So there are nouns like "xerox"
and "laser" that are new nouns.

00:49:59.196 --> 00:50:03.100 align:middle line:84%
They came into the language
comparatively recently.

00:50:03.100 --> 00:50:06.236 align:middle line:84%
When you discover a new thing,
you come up with a name for it,

00:50:06.236 --> 00:50:10.173 align:middle line:84%
and that's a new noun, you add
it to the open class of nouns.

00:50:10.173 --> 00:50:12.976 align:middle line:84%
Or verbs-- there are verbs
like "google" and "fax,"

00:50:12.976 --> 00:50:17.075 align:middle line:84%
or adjectives like "cromulent,"
that have been added

00:50:17.075 --> 00:50:19.182 align:middle line:84%
to the language
comparatively recently,

00:50:19.182 --> 00:50:23.338 align:middle line:84%
as opposed to morphemes like
"in," or "at," or "on"--

00:50:23.338 --> 00:50:28.624 align:middle line:84%
prepositions where it's unclear
that you can make up a new

00:50:28.624 --> 00:50:32.102 align:middle line:84%
preposition or a new
determiner, a word like

00:50:32.102 --> 00:50:35.065 align:middle line:84%
"the" or "an," or a new
auxiliary, a word like "will"

00:50:35.065 --> 00:50:40.971 align:middle line:84%
or "has," in "The professor
has slipped on a banana peel."

00:50:40.971 --> 00:50:44.074 align:middle line:90%
You have that kind of "has."

00:50:44.074 --> 00:50:45.976 align:middle line:84%
We don't seem to
be able to do that.

00:50:45.976 --> 00:50:51.715 align:middle line:84%
Now, we invented new nouns
like "xerox" and "laser,"

00:50:51.715 --> 00:50:53.517 align:middle line:84%
or new verbs like
"google" and "fax,"

00:50:53.517 --> 00:50:58.855 align:middle line:84%
because technology created
new things that we could do.

00:50:58.855 --> 00:51:03.293 align:middle line:84%
I suppose you could imagine
a future world in which, say,

00:51:03.293 --> 00:51:05.562 align:middle line:84%
time travel suddenly
becomes available.

00:51:05.562 --> 00:51:07.931 align:middle line:84%
And then it would be useful
to have an auxiliary that

00:51:07.931 --> 00:51:11.835 align:middle line:84%
means something like "happened
in the past from our viewpoint,

00:51:11.835 --> 00:51:14.171 align:middle line:84%
but from the future from
someone else's viewpoint,"

00:51:14.171 --> 00:51:16.773 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:51:16.773 --> 00:51:18.756 align:middle line:84%
It's unclear whether,
when that happens,

00:51:18.756 --> 00:51:21.458 align:middle line:84%
we will just do that--
make up those auxiliaries--

00:51:21.458 --> 00:51:23.659 align:middle line:84%
or whether we will
be neurologically

00:51:23.659 --> 00:51:26.016 align:middle line:84%
unable to because,
well, auxiliaries

00:51:26.016 --> 00:51:27.317 align:middle line:90%
are closed-class morphemes.

00:51:27.317 --> 00:51:29.686 align:middle line:90%
And you can't make more.

00:51:29.686 --> 00:51:31.188 align:middle line:84%
Interesting open
question that we'll

00:51:31.188 --> 00:51:36.460 align:middle line:90%
try not to talk about anymore.

00:51:36.460 --> 00:51:40.997 align:middle line:90%
All terminology.

00:51:40.997 --> 00:51:43.600 align:middle line:84%
One piece of literary evidence
for the open and closed

00:51:43.600 --> 00:51:46.603 align:middle line:84%
distinction-- this is a
point that my colleague,

00:51:46.603 --> 00:51:49.005 align:middle line:84%
David Pesetsky likes to make
when he teaches this class.

00:51:49.005 --> 00:51:50.841 align:middle line:90%
It's a nice point.

00:51:50.841 --> 00:51:56.012 align:middle line:84%
The poem "Jabberwocky"
from Alice in Wonderland,

00:51:56.012 --> 00:51:59.649 align:middle line:84%
Alice Through the
Looking Glass, is a poem

00:51:59.649 --> 00:52:02.719 align:middle line:90%
which is tricky to understand.

00:52:02.719 --> 00:52:05.055 align:middle line:84%
So many of the words
in it have been

00:52:05.055 --> 00:52:08.125 align:middle line:90%
replaced by nonsense words.

00:52:08.125 --> 00:52:09.583 align:middle line:84%
But crucially, the
words that have

00:52:09.583 --> 00:52:12.019 align:middle line:84%
been replaced by
nonsense words-- they're

00:52:12.019 --> 00:52:14.197 align:middle line:90%
all open-class words.

00:52:14.197 --> 00:52:17.467 align:middle line:84%
So "'Twas brillig, and the
slithy toves did gyre (HARD G)

00:52:17.467 --> 00:52:21.171 align:middle line:84%
and gimble in the-- gyre (SOFT
G) and gimble in the wabe,"

00:52:21.171 --> 00:52:23.940 align:middle line:84%
those blue things,
they're all open class.

00:52:23.940 --> 00:52:28.111 align:middle line:84%
They're nouns, and
verbs, and adjectives,

00:52:28.111 --> 00:52:31.014 align:middle line:84%
so adjectives like
"brillig" and "slithy,"

00:52:31.014 --> 00:52:33.519 align:middle line:84%
nouns like "toves"
and "wabe," and verbs

00:52:33.519 --> 00:52:37.587 align:middle line:90%
like "gyre" and "gimble."

00:52:37.587 --> 00:52:44.227 align:middle line:84%
And the poem only works because
we're open to the possibility

00:52:44.227 --> 00:52:47.297 align:middle line:84%
that there are open-class nouns,
and verbs, and adjectives out

00:52:47.297 --> 00:52:51.935 align:middle line:84%
there that we don't know, so
that's the sense in which we

00:52:51.935 --> 00:52:53.697 align:middle line:90%
can make sense of this poem.

00:52:53.697 --> 00:52:55.739 align:middle line:84%
And you can tell that
these are nouns, and verbs,

00:52:55.739 --> 00:52:56.373 align:middle line:90%
and adjectives.

00:52:56.373 --> 00:52:58.141 align:middle line:90%
Like "toves" looks plural.

00:52:58.141 --> 00:53:01.678 align:middle line:84%
It ends in that same "-s" that
we get in "cats," and so on.

00:53:01.678 --> 00:53:03.613 align:middle line:84%
If you tried to
do "Jabberwocky,"

00:53:03.613 --> 00:53:07.717 align:middle line:84%
but replace the closed-class
morphemes with nonsense--

00:53:07.717 --> 00:53:13.709 align:middle line:84%
so I tried to do
that here-- it's

00:53:13.709 --> 00:53:17.060 align:middle line:84%
also difficult to understand,
but in a different way.

00:53:17.060 --> 00:53:19.396 align:middle line:84%
So "Jabberwocky,"
you read it and you

00:53:19.396 --> 00:53:22.065 align:middle line:84%
think, oh, my vocabulary
must be too small.

00:53:22.065 --> 00:53:24.367 align:middle line:84%
I wish I knew what
these nouns were.

00:53:24.367 --> 00:53:28.071 align:middle line:84%
Whereas this thing, you just
think, oh, the professor

00:53:28.071 --> 00:53:30.874 align:middle line:84%
has some type of
neurological problem.

00:53:30.874 --> 00:53:32.175 align:middle line:90%
Someone should call the police.

00:53:32.175 --> 00:53:34.945 align:middle line:84%
Something terrible
has just happened.

00:53:34.945 --> 00:53:36.479 align:middle line:84%
I won't even try
to read it aloud.

00:53:36.479 --> 00:53:38.281 align:middle line:90%
It would be too upsetting.

00:53:38.281 --> 00:53:41.064 align:middle line:84%
So all I've done here is to
try to go through-- so all

00:53:41.064 --> 00:53:43.253 align:middle line:84%
of the adjectives, and
nouns, and verbs-- there

00:53:43.253 --> 00:53:48.758 align:middle line:84%
are real ones, and I've just
replaced the auxiliaries,

00:53:48.758 --> 00:53:51.094 align:middle line:84%
and the word for and,
and the prepositions,

00:53:51.094 --> 00:53:51.995 align:middle line:90%
and the terminators.

00:53:51.995 --> 00:53:53.763 align:middle line:84%
I've replaced those
with nonsense.

00:53:53.763 --> 00:53:56.199 align:middle line:84%
And the result of
that is disturbing.

00:53:56.199 --> 00:53:58.835 align:middle line:84%
It's not the same
as "Jabberwocky."

00:53:58.835 --> 00:54:00.837 align:middle line:84%
That's a reason to
take seriously the idea

00:54:00.837 --> 00:54:03.273 align:middle line:84%
that open-class and
closed-class morphemes--

00:54:03.273 --> 00:54:06.243 align:middle line:84%
they're different in a way
that something in our brain

00:54:06.243 --> 00:54:12.048 align:middle line:90%
cares about deeply.

00:54:12.048 --> 00:54:12.983 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:54:12.983 --> 00:54:15.685 align:middle line:90%
So what's in our lexicon?

00:54:15.685 --> 00:54:21.024 align:middle line:84%
We have these entries that
have information about sound.

00:54:21.024 --> 00:54:22.559 align:middle line:90%
So here's a word.

00:54:22.559 --> 00:54:24.861 align:middle line:90%
It's pronounced "cat."

00:54:24.861 --> 00:54:28.665 align:middle line:84%
We're going to talk later
about how to talk carefully

00:54:28.665 --> 00:54:30.767 align:middle line:84%
about how to pronounce
words, like what exactly

00:54:30.767 --> 00:54:33.870 align:middle line:84%
you're saying when you say
that it's pronounced "cat."

00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:36.673 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, so one of the things
that's known about this word

00:54:36.673 --> 00:54:37.941 align:middle line:90%
is that it's pronounced "cat."

00:54:37.941 --> 00:54:40.343 align:middle line:84%
Something about its
meaning-- whatever.

00:54:40.343 --> 00:54:41.978 align:middle line:84%
I showed you a picture
of a cat before.

00:54:41.978 --> 00:54:46.683 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that was better
than what I've done here.

00:54:46.683 --> 00:54:48.135 align:middle line:84%
And then the kinds
of information

00:54:48.135 --> 00:54:49.552 align:middle line:84%
that I've been
talking about-- you

00:54:49.552 --> 00:54:53.523 align:middle line:84%
have to list whether the
morpheme is bound or free.

00:54:53.523 --> 00:54:55.225 align:middle line:84%
Because that's not
the kind of thing

00:54:55.225 --> 00:54:58.695 align:middle line:84%
that is uniform across
languages, necessarily.

00:54:58.695 --> 00:55:02.232 align:middle line:84%
So English, for example--
we've now several times

00:55:02.232 --> 00:55:06.002 align:middle line:84%
said that the plural morpheme
is a suffix, an affix.

00:55:06.002 --> 00:55:07.237 align:middle line:90%
It's a bound morpheme.

00:55:07.237 --> 00:55:09.572 align:middle line:84%
It needs to be
attached to something.

00:55:09.572 --> 00:55:13.247 align:middle line:84%
In Tagalog, the plural
morpheme is pronounced "mga."

00:55:13.247 --> 00:55:16.012 align:middle line:84%
It's the boldfaced
morpheme there.

00:55:16.012 --> 00:55:17.547 align:middle line:90%
And it's not a bound morpheme.

00:55:17.547 --> 00:55:20.317 align:middle line:84%
It's a freestanding
word, so it doesn't have

00:55:20.317 --> 00:55:21.584 align:middle line:90%
to be attached to anything.

00:55:21.584 --> 00:55:24.054 align:middle line:84%
So the way you say "big
bananas" in Tagalog

00:55:24.054 --> 00:55:25.822 align:middle line:90%
is "mga malalaking saging."

00:55:25.822 --> 00:55:27.424 align:middle line:90%
"Mga" is this freestanding word.

00:55:27.424 --> 00:55:28.992 align:middle line:84%
It's at the beginning
of that phrase.

00:55:28.992 --> 00:55:31.795 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have to be anywhere
near the word for banana.

00:55:31.795 --> 00:55:34.130 align:middle line:84%
So it's not attached
to anything.

00:55:34.130 --> 00:55:37.534 align:middle line:84%
Tagalog orthography is mostly
fairly straightforward.

00:55:37.534 --> 00:55:39.202 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the
two words in Tagalog

00:55:39.202 --> 00:55:41.856 align:middle line:84%
that's not spelled more or
less the way it's pronounced.

00:55:41.856 --> 00:55:43.106 align:middle line:90%
So that's the plural morpheme.

00:55:43.106 --> 00:55:49.612 align:middle line:84%
It's pronounced "manga," this
thing that's spelled M-G-A.

00:55:49.612 --> 00:55:53.016 align:middle line:84%
So you need to--
for a lexical entry,

00:55:53.016 --> 00:55:54.851 align:middle line:84%
a word that's in
your lexicon, we're

00:55:54.851 --> 00:55:57.287 align:middle line:84%
going to need to list how it's
pronounced, what it means,

00:55:57.287 --> 00:55:58.988 align:middle line:90%
whether it's bound or free.

00:55:58.988 --> 00:56:01.885 align:middle line:84%
Here's another example
of languages varying

00:56:01.885 --> 00:56:03.927 align:middle line:84%
with respect to whether
things are bound or free.

00:56:03.927 --> 00:56:06.863 align:middle line:84%
In English, the word
"friend" is a free morpheme.

00:56:06.863 --> 00:56:10.600 align:middle line:84%
In Passamaquoddy, the word for
"friend" is a bound morpheme.

00:56:10.600 --> 00:56:12.235 align:middle line:84%
It needs to be
attached to something.

00:56:12.235 --> 00:56:14.604 align:middle line:84%
So in English, you
say, "my friend."

00:56:14.604 --> 00:56:17.307 align:middle line:84%
The Passamaquoddy word
for "my friend" is "itap."

00:56:17.307 --> 00:56:20.777 align:middle line:84%
There isn't a
Passamaquoddy word "itap,"

00:56:20.777 --> 00:56:24.114 align:middle line:84%
so you have to add a prefix
to the word for "friend"

00:56:24.114 --> 00:56:28.418 align:middle line:84%
in Passamaquoddy, indicating
whose friend it is.

00:56:28.418 --> 00:56:30.954 align:middle line:84%
It can't just be
a word by itself.

00:56:30.954 --> 00:56:33.390 align:middle line:84%
So it's bound, needs to be
attached to something else.

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:35.892 align:middle line:90%
It's not a complete word.

00:56:35.892 --> 00:56:38.728 align:middle line:84%
So the lexicon of
a given language

00:56:38.728 --> 00:56:40.730 align:middle line:84%
has to indicate whether
particular morphemes are

00:56:40.730 --> 00:56:44.033 align:middle line:90%
bound or free.

00:56:44.033 --> 00:56:45.935 align:middle line:84%
It has to indicate
whether morphemes

00:56:45.935 --> 00:56:47.637 align:middle line:90%
are prefixes or suffixes.

00:56:47.637 --> 00:56:49.672 align:middle line:84%
So for example,
English has suffixes

00:56:49.672 --> 00:56:51.141 align:middle line:90%
indicating the past tense.

00:56:51.141 --> 00:56:52.742 align:middle line:84%
So we have the verb
"dance," and it

00:56:52.742 --> 00:56:57.447 align:middle line:84%
has a past tense "danced," where
we add this D to make it past.

00:56:57.447 --> 00:57:00.850 align:middle line:84%
Lardil has prefixes
that indicate that verbs

00:57:00.850 --> 00:57:01.785 align:middle line:90%
are in the past tense.

00:57:01.785 --> 00:57:03.286 align:middle line:84%
So the verb "to
dance" is "luuli,"

00:57:03.286 --> 00:57:04.754 align:middle line:84%
and the past tense
is "yuud-luuli,"

00:57:04.754 --> 00:57:08.391 align:middle line:84%
so there's a prefix "yuud-" that
you add to the verb to make it

00:57:08.391 --> 00:57:10.193 align:middle line:90%
past.

00:57:10.193 --> 00:57:13.163 align:middle line:84%
So you have to indicate whether
morphemes are bound or free,

00:57:13.163 --> 00:57:16.166 align:middle line:84%
whether they're prefixes, or
suffixes, or something else.

00:57:16.166 --> 00:57:19.736 align:middle line:84%
So languages can vary with
respect to what kind of affixes

00:57:19.736 --> 00:57:22.939 align:middle line:84%
they have, and where they
go, and what they mean.

00:57:22.939 --> 00:57:26.910 align:middle line:84%
And morphology is the study
of the rules governing

00:57:26.910 --> 00:57:28.411 align:middle line:90%
this kind of variability.

00:57:28.411 --> 00:57:30.713 align:middle line:84%
And that's what we're going
to start the semester with,

00:57:30.713 --> 00:57:33.950 align:middle line:84%
is spending some time
studying morphology.

00:57:33.950 --> 00:57:36.052 align:middle line:84%
Part of the work
of morphology is

00:57:36.052 --> 00:57:39.556 align:middle line:84%
doing morphological analysis
of languages that are not

00:57:39.556 --> 00:57:43.726 align:middle line:84%
familiar, so
looking at data sets

00:57:43.726 --> 00:57:46.729 align:middle line:84%
and trying to piece together
what all the morphemes are,

00:57:46.729 --> 00:57:48.832 align:middle line:84%
trying to separate
out the morphemes

00:57:48.832 --> 00:57:51.768 align:middle line:84%
and figure out
what they all mean.

00:57:51.768 --> 00:57:54.404 align:middle line:84%
You will surely be given
problem sets in which you

00:57:54.404 --> 00:57:55.982 align:middle line:90%
are asked to do this.

00:57:55.982 --> 00:57:58.357 align:middle line:84%
So I wanted-- I think this is
what I'm doing next-- yeah,

00:57:58.357 --> 00:58:01.110 align:middle line:84%
I wanted to show you
a data set of the kind

00:58:01.110 --> 00:58:03.613 align:middle line:84%
that we might ask
you to deal with.

00:58:03.613 --> 00:58:06.382 align:middle line:84%
And let's work
through it together

00:58:06.382 --> 00:58:09.385 align:middle line:84%
and try to figure out what
the different morphemes are.

00:58:09.385 --> 00:58:11.855 align:middle line:84%
So here are a bunch
of verbs in Swahili.

00:58:11.855 --> 00:58:15.124 align:middle line:90%
Anybody speak Swahili?

00:58:15.124 --> 00:58:15.625 align:middle line:90%
Kind of?

00:58:15.625 --> 00:58:16.359 align:middle line:90%
Sort of?

00:58:16.359 --> 00:58:19.762 align:middle line:84%
Try to suppress your knowledge
of Swahili for a second.

00:58:19.762 --> 00:58:24.033 align:middle line:84%
Nobody ask her what
the answers are.

00:58:24.033 --> 00:58:26.469 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch of
verbs in Swahili.

00:58:26.469 --> 00:58:28.938 align:middle line:84%
The first thing you want to do
is look through all of these

00:58:28.938 --> 00:58:33.443 align:middle line:84%
and look for words that
have something in common

00:58:33.443 --> 00:58:36.579 align:middle line:84%
in their meaning,
and also seem--

00:58:36.579 --> 00:58:38.882 align:middle line:84%
and then try to figure
out which part of the word

00:58:38.882 --> 00:58:43.553 align:middle line:84%
is the morpheme that
has that meaning.

00:58:43.553 --> 00:58:45.722 align:middle line:84%
This is where I
wish it was easier

00:58:45.722 --> 00:58:47.357 align:middle line:84%
to show you things
on a blackboard.

00:58:47.357 --> 00:58:52.996 align:middle line:84%
But does anybody see a
morpheme in this list?

00:58:52.996 --> 00:58:54.264 align:middle line:90%
Oh, many people see morphemes.

00:58:54.264 --> 00:58:54.797 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:58:54.797 --> 00:58:56.359 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: "ni" tends
to indicate "I."

00:58:56.359 --> 00:58:58.401 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: "ni"
looks like it means "I."

00:58:58.401 --> 00:59:01.471 align:middle line:84%
So we've got "ni" in the first
one, "ni" in the third one,

00:59:01.471 --> 00:59:04.741 align:middle line:84%
"ni" in the fourth one,
"ni" in "I will get it."

00:59:04.741 --> 00:59:06.142 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, a whole bunch of "ni"s.

00:59:06.142 --> 00:59:07.544 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, sounds good.

00:59:07.544 --> 00:59:09.245 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:59:09.245 --> 00:59:11.073 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:11.073 --> 00:59:12.448 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Say it again.

00:59:12.448 --> 00:59:14.678 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:14.678 --> 00:59:16.553 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Oh,
yes, the verb "got"--

00:59:16.553 --> 00:59:19.522 align:middle line:84%
that's absolutely right--
looks like it's "pata."

00:59:19.522 --> 00:59:21.925 align:middle line:84%
So all of the verbs
that have "got"

00:59:21.925 --> 00:59:24.093 align:middle line:84%
as part of their meaning--
if you look through this,

00:59:24.093 --> 00:59:27.430 align:middle line:84%
I think there are only two
verbs here-- there's "got"

00:59:27.430 --> 00:59:28.831 align:middle line:90%
and there's "hit."

00:59:28.831 --> 00:59:30.667 align:middle line:84%
And "got" looks
like it's "pata."

00:59:30.667 --> 00:59:32.035 align:middle line:90%
What's "hit?"

00:59:32.035 --> 00:59:33.236 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: "piga."

00:59:33.236 --> 00:59:34.938 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
"piga," yeah, good.

00:59:34.938 --> 00:59:36.839 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:59:36.839 --> 00:59:39.642 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Actually, it seems like
"pata" is more [INAUDIBLE]....

00:59:39.642 --> 00:59:46.601 align:middle line:84%
And then there's "li"
versus [INAUDIBLE]....

00:59:46.601 --> 00:59:48.184 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
So there's a "li."

00:59:48.184 --> 00:59:49.419 align:middle line:90%
So you're seeing a "li."

00:59:49.419 --> 00:59:50.687 align:middle line:90%
Where are you seeing a "li"?

00:59:50.687 --> 00:59:54.857 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:54.857 --> 00:59:59.041 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: So you
get "nilipata" for "I got"

00:59:59.041 --> 01:00:03.614 align:middle line:84%
and "nita"-- do we see
nitapaka or nita-- oh,

01:00:03.614 --> 01:00:07.103 align:middle line:84%
we've got "nitakipata" for
"I will get it," don't we?

01:00:07.103 --> 01:00:08.404 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:00:08.404 --> 01:00:10.373 align:middle line:90%
Sounds good.

01:00:10.373 --> 01:00:12.241 align:middle line:84%
Let me move forward
on the slide.

01:00:12.241 --> 01:00:13.142 align:middle line:90%
So this is great.

01:00:13.142 --> 01:00:15.278 align:middle line:84%
People are doing exactly
what you want to do.

01:00:15.278 --> 01:00:16.379 align:middle line:84%
You can look through
all of this stuff,

01:00:16.379 --> 01:00:18.147 align:middle line:84%
look for patterns
that you seem to see,

01:00:18.147 --> 01:00:23.052 align:middle line:84%
try to isolate those patterns
to figure out what's going on.

01:00:23.052 --> 01:00:26.456 align:middle line:84%
So one way to do this would be
to start by finding the verb

01:00:26.456 --> 01:00:27.056 align:middle line:90%
stems.

01:00:27.056 --> 01:00:29.959 align:middle line:84%
I deliberately jumbled the
presentation at the beginning,

01:00:29.959 --> 01:00:34.097 align:middle line:90%
just put them in a random order.

01:00:34.097 --> 01:00:36.098 align:middle line:84%
But one thing to
do, and you guys

01:00:36.098 --> 01:00:38.746 align:middle line:84%
didn't need me to do
this for you-- you did it

01:00:38.746 --> 01:00:40.766 align:middle line:84%
in your heads-- you make
yourself a list of all

01:00:40.766 --> 01:00:42.380 align:middle line:84%
the words that
have "get" or "got"

01:00:42.380 --> 01:00:44.207 align:middle line:84%
and all the words
that have "hit."

01:00:44.207 --> 01:00:46.542 align:middle line:84%
And then you would
discover that "pata"

01:00:46.542 --> 01:00:51.047 align:middle line:84%
is the one for "get" and
"piga" is the one for "hit."

01:00:51.047 --> 01:00:56.486 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe similarly, keep
sorting them by their affixes.

01:00:56.486 --> 01:00:59.055 align:middle line:84%
And if you kept doing that, you
would discover that there was

01:00:59.055 --> 01:01:02.025 align:middle line:84%
a subject prefix at the
beginning that's either "ni-"

01:01:02.025 --> 01:01:04.160 align:middle line:90%
or "u-" or "wa-."

01:01:04.160 --> 01:01:09.932 align:middle line:84%
And then, that there is a "li-"
prefix for past and a "ta-"

01:01:09.932 --> 01:01:14.270 align:middle line:84%
prefix for future, and that
there are also prefixes

01:01:14.270 --> 01:01:17.407 align:middle line:84%
for the objects, "ki-" and
"ku-" and "tu-" and "wa-."

01:01:17.407 --> 01:01:19.909 align:middle line:84%
So if we kept doing
what we were just doing,

01:01:19.909 --> 01:01:21.577 align:middle line:84%
this is where we'd
eventually end up.

01:01:21.577 --> 01:01:24.747 align:middle line:84%
That's the analysis
we would end up with.

01:01:24.747 --> 01:01:25.882 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

01:01:25.882 --> 01:01:26.876 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:01:26.876 --> 01:01:28.584 align:middle line:84%
That's what we would
be asking you to do.

01:01:28.584 --> 01:01:30.987 align:middle line:84%
We will surely ask you to
do this on a problem set.

01:01:30.987 --> 01:01:33.589 align:middle line:84%
So we'll give you a bunch of
data, and say, go through this.

01:01:33.589 --> 01:01:35.224 align:middle line:90%
Find all the morphemes.

01:01:35.224 --> 01:01:41.148 align:middle line:84%
And that's the kind of
thing we'd want you to do.

01:01:41.148 --> 01:01:42.565 align:middle line:84%
If there's anybody
who is secretly

01:01:42.565 --> 01:01:45.435 align:middle line:84%
thinking to themselves, I have
no idea what just happened,

01:01:45.435 --> 01:01:48.705 align:middle line:84%
that made no sense to
me at all-- don't panic.

01:01:48.705 --> 01:01:53.409 align:middle line:84%
Send me email or talk to
your TA during recitation.

01:01:53.409 --> 01:01:54.911 align:middle line:84%
This is the kind
of thing that we'll

01:01:54.911 --> 01:02:01.317 align:middle line:84%
try to practice some before
you have to do it for real.

01:02:01.317 --> 01:02:03.886 align:middle line:84%
And then the thing to
do-- test yourself.

01:02:03.886 --> 01:02:06.599 align:middle line:84%
How do you say,
"They will get us?"

01:02:06.599 --> 01:02:08.057 align:middle line:84%
How do you say,
"They will get us?"

01:02:08.057 --> 01:02:11.060 align:middle line:90%
So that was Swahili.

01:02:11.060 --> 01:02:13.362 align:middle line:84%
The Swahili involved
some verbs that had

01:02:13.362 --> 01:02:14.797 align:middle line:90%
a bunch of prefixes on them.

01:02:14.797 --> 01:02:18.000 align:middle line:84%
And we have mostly been talking
about prefixes and suffixes

01:02:18.000 --> 01:02:22.004 align:middle line:84%
as the kinds of affixes
that you add to things.

01:02:22.004 --> 01:02:24.807 align:middle line:84%
Just as a warning, there
are other kinds of affixes

01:02:24.807 --> 01:02:26.175 align:middle line:90%
out there in the world.

01:02:26.175 --> 01:02:28.478 align:middle line:84%
So I want to show you some
of the other kinds of things

01:02:28.478 --> 01:02:30.279 align:middle line:90%
that exist.

01:02:30.279 --> 01:02:33.015 align:middle line:84%
Tagalog is kind of
famous for having

01:02:33.015 --> 01:02:34.650 align:middle line:90%
what are called "infixes."

01:02:34.650 --> 01:02:39.756 align:middle line:84%
So it has these
morphemes that sometimes

01:02:39.756 --> 01:02:42.825 align:middle line:90%
go in the middle of words.

01:02:42.825 --> 01:02:45.828 align:middle line:84%
We've got a bunch
of Tagalog words.

01:02:45.828 --> 01:02:48.364 align:middle line:90%
They're all in the past tense.

01:02:48.364 --> 01:02:52.802 align:middle line:84%
They all contain a
past tense morpheme.

01:02:52.802 --> 01:02:55.289 align:middle line:90%
What's the past tense morpheme?

01:02:55.289 --> 01:02:55.872 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: "um."

01:02:55.872 --> 01:02:57.840 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
"um," yes, it's "um."

01:02:57.840 --> 01:02:58.508 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

01:02:58.508 --> 01:02:59.642 align:middle line:90%
So you see it.

01:02:59.642 --> 01:03:01.811 align:middle line:84%
I think I highlighted
it on the next slide.

01:03:01.811 --> 01:03:03.946 align:middle line:84%
I hope I was smart
enough to do that.

01:03:03.946 --> 01:03:05.615 align:middle line:90%
Yes, there we go.

01:03:05.615 --> 01:03:07.917 align:middle line:90%
So Tagalog has infixes.

01:03:07.917 --> 01:03:12.588 align:middle line:90%
"um" goes-- where does it go?

01:03:12.588 --> 01:03:14.423 align:middle line:84%
What's the rule
for where it goes?

01:03:14.423 --> 01:03:14.957 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:03:14.957 --> 01:03:25.193 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:25.193 --> 01:03:26.235 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:03:26.235 --> 01:03:27.236 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Before the first vowel.

01:03:27.236 --> 01:03:28.638 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Before
the first vowel is another way

01:03:28.638 --> 01:03:30.072 align:middle line:90%
to say the same thing, yeah.

01:03:30.072 --> 01:03:33.278 align:middle line:84%
So in the words that
start with vowels,

01:03:33.278 --> 01:03:35.611 align:middle line:84%
it goes at the beginning
because that's the first vowel.

01:03:35.611 --> 01:03:37.280 align:middle line:84%
In the words that
start with consonants,

01:03:37.280 --> 01:03:38.981 align:middle line:84%
well, it goes before
the first vowel.

01:03:38.981 --> 01:03:39.582 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

01:03:39.582 --> 01:03:42.151 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that works.

01:03:42.151 --> 01:03:44.020 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that works for Tagalog.

01:03:44.020 --> 01:03:49.325 align:middle line:84%
Now, we have to be careful
when we say what an infix is.

01:03:49.325 --> 01:03:52.189 align:middle line:84%
So let me try to be
careful about it.

01:03:52.189 --> 01:03:54.397 align:middle line:84%
Because I think I was careless
about it a second ago.

01:03:54.397 --> 01:03:55.898 align:middle line:90%
So let me be careful.

01:03:55.898 --> 01:04:01.158 align:middle line:84%
An infix is an affix for
which the rules say something

01:04:01.158 --> 01:04:04.588 align:middle line:84%
like-- the rules for where it
goes-- say something like, put

01:04:04.588 --> 01:04:07.514 align:middle line:84%
it before the first
vowel, or put it

01:04:07.514 --> 01:04:11.097 align:middle line:84%
after the first consonant,
or sometimes, put it

01:04:11.097 --> 01:04:13.249 align:middle line:90%
after the first syllable.

01:04:13.249 --> 01:04:16.519 align:middle line:84%
A prefix is an affix for
which the rule says, put it

01:04:16.519 --> 01:04:18.187 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning.

01:04:18.187 --> 01:04:20.857 align:middle line:84%
A suffix says,
put it at the end.

01:04:20.857 --> 01:04:24.687 align:middle line:84%
That's what infixes and
prefixes and suffixes are.

01:04:24.687 --> 01:04:26.562 align:middle line:84%
And I'm being careful
about this because this

01:04:26.562 --> 01:04:28.998 align:middle line:84%
is something people
sometimes get confused about.

01:04:28.998 --> 01:04:32.568 align:middle line:84%
A Swahili verb, like
"I will get you there,"

01:04:32.568 --> 01:04:35.805 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have any
infixes in it at all.

01:04:35.805 --> 01:04:39.850 align:middle line:84%
It's got a verb, "pata," which
is preceded by three prefixes,

01:04:39.850 --> 01:04:44.342 align:middle line:84%
each of which was put--
the rule for "ku-" says,

01:04:44.342 --> 01:04:47.116 align:middle line:90%
put me right before the verb.

01:04:47.116 --> 01:04:51.087 align:middle line:84%
And the rule for "ta-" says,
put me right before "ku-."

01:04:51.087 --> 01:04:53.689 align:middle line:84%
And the rule for "ni-" says,
put me at the beginning.

01:04:53.689 --> 01:04:55.858 align:middle line:90%
Those are all prefixes.

01:04:55.858 --> 01:05:00.630 align:middle line:84%
So none of them say, put
me inside something else.

01:05:00.630 --> 01:05:01.898 align:middle line:90%
None of those are infixes.

01:05:01.898 --> 01:05:02.932 align:middle line:90%
They're all prefixes.

01:05:02.932 --> 01:05:06.002 align:middle line:84%
And I'm saying that slowly and
carefully because by the time

01:05:06.002 --> 01:05:08.271 align:middle line:84%
you're done with all
these prefixes, well,

01:05:08.271 --> 01:05:10.907 align:middle line:84%
they aren't all at the
beginning of the word.

01:05:10.907 --> 01:05:14.243 align:middle line:84%
There's a string of prefixes,
but they're all prefixes.

01:05:14.243 --> 01:05:16.078 align:middle line:84%
Does that make sense,
the distinction

01:05:16.078 --> 01:05:18.232 align:middle line:84%
that I am drawing
between prefixes that,

01:05:18.232 --> 01:05:20.149 align:middle line:84%
through no fault of their
own, find themselves

01:05:20.149 --> 01:05:21.584 align:middle line:84%
not at the beginning
of the word,

01:05:21.584 --> 01:05:24.687 align:middle line:84%
and infixes which have
rules like, put me

01:05:24.687 --> 01:05:25.788 align:middle line:90%
before the first vowel.

01:05:25.788 --> 01:05:29.125 align:middle line:84%
I don't care where
the word begins.

01:05:29.125 --> 01:05:31.327 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:05:31.327 --> 01:05:33.629 align:middle line:84%
That's how we distinguish
these things from each other.

01:05:33.629 --> 01:05:35.131 align:middle line:84%
And as I say, I'm
going through it

01:05:35.131 --> 01:05:37.099 align:middle line:84%
slowly because it's
something that's confusing.

01:05:37.099 --> 01:05:39.068 align:middle line:84%
It's something people
get confused by.

01:05:39.068 --> 01:05:46.175 align:middle line:84%
So if you're feeling
confused, speak up.

01:05:46.175 --> 01:05:47.410 align:middle line:90%
Tagalog has infixes.

01:05:47.410 --> 01:05:49.779 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
like Egyptian Arabic,

01:05:49.779 --> 01:05:54.650 align:middle line:84%
not every Arabic, that have
what are called templates.

01:05:54.650 --> 01:05:58.788 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch of verbs
in Egyptian Arabic involving

01:05:58.788 --> 01:06:01.757 align:middle line:90%
"live" and "enter."

01:06:01.757 --> 01:06:05.161 align:middle line:90%
Anybody here speak Arabic?

01:06:05.161 --> 01:06:07.496 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, OK, so again, try
to suppress your knowledge

01:06:07.496 --> 01:06:12.068 align:middle line:90%
of Arabic for just a second.

01:06:12.068 --> 01:06:18.874 align:middle line:84%
What do you think the
morpheme is that means live?

01:06:18.874 --> 01:06:22.712 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Maybe S, K, vowel, N.

01:06:22.712 --> 01:06:24.981 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: So
what do those verbs all

01:06:24.981 --> 01:06:27.817 align:middle line:90%
have in common, those forms?

01:06:27.817 --> 01:06:35.563 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, they all have S-K-N. Or
enter-- those all have D, X, L.

01:06:35.563 --> 01:06:40.606 align:middle line:90%
Now X is that sound.

01:06:40.606 --> 01:06:42.898 align:middle line:84%
This is something the Semitic
languages are famous for.

01:06:42.898 --> 01:06:45.701 align:middle line:84%
They have what are
called triliteral roots,

01:06:45.701 --> 01:06:50.206 align:middle line:84%
verbs for which the morpheme
is just a string of consonants,

01:06:50.206 --> 01:06:51.607 align:middle line:90%
and usually three.

01:06:51.607 --> 01:06:55.978 align:middle line:90%
There are verbs that have two.

01:06:55.978 --> 01:06:57.860 align:middle line:84%
And the other
morphemes-- the morphemes

01:06:57.860 --> 01:07:01.747 align:middle line:84%
that tell you things like
tense, and who the subject is,

01:07:01.747 --> 01:07:05.830 align:middle line:84%
and things like that--
are often vowels that you

01:07:05.830 --> 01:07:10.092 align:middle line:84%
put in between the constants
that make up the verb.

01:07:10.092 --> 01:07:14.096 align:middle line:84%
It's called
templatic morphology.

01:07:14.096 --> 01:07:19.035 align:middle line:84%
So S-K-N is to live in,
and D-X-L is to enter.

01:07:19.035 --> 01:07:22.971 align:middle line:84%
And past tense with the third
person subject is two a's.

01:07:22.971 --> 01:07:25.408 align:middle line:84%
So you get verbs like
"sakan" and "daxal."

01:07:25.408 --> 01:07:26.709 align:middle line:90%
I'm probably saying them badly.

01:07:26.709 --> 01:07:28.377 align:middle line:84%
Do you want to
pronounce them for us?

01:07:28.377 --> 01:07:29.578 align:middle line:84%
I'm probably not
pronouncing them right.

01:07:29.578 --> 01:07:30.279 align:middle line:90%
Can you pronounce them?

01:07:30.279 --> 01:07:30.880 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

01:07:30.880 --> 01:07:32.255 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Say it again.

01:07:32.255 --> 01:07:35.651 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:35.651 --> 01:07:37.053 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Cool.

01:07:37.053 --> 01:07:42.058 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so that's how this
type of morphology works.

01:07:42.058 --> 01:07:44.386 align:middle line:84%
Tagalog again-- Tagalog
has another kind

01:07:44.386 --> 01:07:45.594 align:middle line:90%
of morphology, which is cool.

01:07:45.594 --> 01:07:47.229 align:middle line:90%
It's called reduplication.

01:07:47.229 --> 01:07:49.865 align:middle line:84%
This is how you form the future
tense of certain types of verbs

01:07:49.865 --> 01:07:50.566 align:middle line:90%
in Tagalog.

01:07:50.566 --> 01:07:53.169 align:middle line:84%
So the future of
"swim" is "lalangoy."

01:07:53.169 --> 01:07:55.204 align:middle line:90%
The future of "eat" is "kakain."

01:07:55.204 --> 01:07:58.040 align:middle line:84%
The future of "become
tall" is tataas.

01:07:58.040 --> 01:08:02.311 align:middle line:84%
What's the future
morpheme in Tagalog?

01:08:02.311 --> 01:08:03.538 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:03.538 --> 01:08:04.580 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:08:04.580 --> 01:08:07.316 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:07.316 --> 01:08:08.984 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS:
Yeah, it's like copy

01:08:08.984 --> 01:08:12.922 align:middle line:90%
the first consonant and vowel.

01:08:12.922 --> 01:08:17.725 align:middle line:84%
That's how you do
futures in Tagalog.

01:08:17.725 --> 01:08:19.327 align:middle line:84%
It's specifically
the first consonant.

01:08:19.327 --> 01:08:24.433 align:middle line:84%
And Tagalog does have syllables
that end with consonants.

01:08:24.433 --> 01:08:34.510 align:middle line:84%
And you don't copy the consonant
at the end of the syllable.

01:08:34.510 --> 01:08:36.145 align:middle line:84%
But Tagalog has
several different kinds

01:08:36.145 --> 01:08:37.212 align:middle line:90%
of reduplication.

01:08:37.212 --> 01:08:38.881 align:middle line:90%
Here's another one.

01:08:38.881 --> 01:08:41.903 align:middle line:84%
If you want to say that
something is rather adjective,

01:08:41.903 --> 01:08:44.153 align:middle line:84%
there are a bunch of adjectives
that start with "ma-."

01:08:44.153 --> 01:08:46.778 align:middle line:84%
Not all adjectives do, but there
are a bunch of adjectives that

01:08:46.778 --> 01:08:48.090 align:middle line:90%
start with a prefix "ma-."

01:08:48.090 --> 01:08:50.893 align:middle line:84%
And then the way to say that
something is rather adjective

01:08:50.893 --> 01:08:55.096 align:middle line:84%
is to reduplicate the first
two syllables of the adjective.

01:08:55.096 --> 01:08:57.265 align:middle line:84%
If the adjective is
only two syllables long,

01:08:57.265 --> 01:08:58.834 align:middle line:84%
then you reduplicate
the whole thing.

01:08:58.834 --> 01:09:00.803 align:middle line:84%
So you get "mataas-taas"
is "rather tall,"

01:09:00.803 --> 01:09:04.372 align:middle line:84%
or "malapit-lapit"
is "rather close."

01:09:04.372 --> 01:09:07.042 align:middle line:84%
If it's longer than that, then
you just copy the first two.

01:09:07.042 --> 01:09:12.581 align:middle line:84%
So you get "matali-talino"
for "rather intelligent."

01:09:12.581 --> 01:09:15.317 align:middle line:84%
So reduplicative
morphemes-- there

01:09:15.317 --> 01:09:17.586 align:middle line:84%
are three or four different
types of reduplication

01:09:17.586 --> 01:09:18.854 align:middle line:90%
in Tagalog.

01:09:18.854 --> 01:09:22.658 align:middle line:84%
You've got a morpheme
which is a prefix,

01:09:22.658 --> 01:09:27.328 align:middle line:84%
and it is sort of specified
for phonological content,

01:09:27.328 --> 01:09:31.033 align:middle line:90%
but just how many syllables.

01:09:31.033 --> 01:09:34.030 align:middle line:84%
Most prefixes,
you've got a prefix,

01:09:34.030 --> 01:09:35.738 align:middle line:84%
and it's got consonants
and vowels in it,

01:09:35.738 --> 01:09:37.038 align:middle line:90%
and you put that before.

01:09:37.038 --> 01:09:40.676 align:middle line:84%
You get a English prefix,
like "un-" for "unhappy"--

01:09:40.676 --> 01:09:41.977 align:middle line:90%
that's a prefix.

01:09:41.977 --> 01:09:43.345 align:middle line:90%
It means whatever it means.

01:09:43.345 --> 01:09:47.917 align:middle line:84%
And it consists of the vowel,
"uh," and consonant, N.

01:09:47.917 --> 01:09:49.852 align:middle line:84%
And you put that
before adjectives

01:09:49.852 --> 01:09:53.822 align:middle line:84%
to make other adjectives
that mean not adjective.

01:09:53.822 --> 01:09:57.660 align:middle line:84%
Reduplicative morphemes are
also prefixes or suffixes,

01:09:57.660 --> 01:09:59.355 align:middle line:84%
but they don't have
any specification

01:09:59.355 --> 01:10:01.230 align:middle line:84%
as to what consonants
and vowels are in them.

01:10:01.230 --> 01:10:03.199 align:middle line:84%
You just copy whatever
is already there.

01:10:03.199 --> 01:10:09.038 align:middle line:84%
That's how reduplication
seems to work.

01:10:09.038 --> 01:10:10.472 align:middle line:84%
When I was first
studying Tagalog,

01:10:10.472 --> 01:10:17.746 align:middle line:84%
we had contests to see who
could repeat the most syllables.

01:10:17.746 --> 01:10:21.183 align:middle line:84%
I remember one that we tried
to pass off on our teacher

01:10:21.183 --> 01:10:26.789 align:middle line:84%
was [SPEAKING TAGALOG] which
means, "they are regretting it

01:10:26.789 --> 01:10:30.392 align:middle line:84%
a little bit all the time,"
or something like that.

01:10:30.392 --> 01:10:34.863 align:middle line:84%
There's a Tagalog
joke Tagalogs like

01:10:34.863 --> 01:10:39.101 align:middle line:84%
to tell about Filipinos
who are in an elevator,

01:10:39.101 --> 01:10:42.137 align:middle line:84%
and there there's
an American who's

01:10:42.137 --> 01:10:44.873 align:middle line:84%
also in the elevator who is
astonished when the elevator

01:10:44.873 --> 01:10:50.879 align:middle line:84%
doors open, and one of the
Filipinos, who's outside,

01:10:50.879 --> 01:10:56.255 align:middle line:84%
asks the other,
"babalaba," which

01:10:56.255 --> 01:11:04.360 align:middle line:84%
involves the verb "baba," which
is "to go down," reduplication,

01:11:04.360 --> 01:11:07.496 align:middle line:84%
and the question
particle, which is "ba."

01:11:07.496 --> 01:11:11.433 align:middle line:90%
So going down is "babalaba."

01:11:11.433 --> 01:11:13.135 align:middle line:84%
Tagalogs like the
idea of an American

01:11:13.135 --> 01:11:17.172 align:middle line:84%
having to react to
that conversation.

01:11:17.172 --> 01:11:22.811 align:middle line:90%
So how are we doing?

01:11:22.811 --> 01:11:26.982 align:middle line:84%
So this is all a little tour
through non-intuitive kinds

01:11:26.982 --> 01:11:28.117 align:middle line:90%
of morphemes.

01:11:28.117 --> 01:11:29.251 align:middle line:90%
So yes, there are prefixes.

01:11:29.251 --> 01:11:30.219 align:middle line:90%
Yes, there are suffixes.

01:11:30.219 --> 01:11:31.387 align:middle line:84%
But there are other
kinds of morphemes

01:11:31.387 --> 01:11:32.354 align:middle line:90%
out there in the world.

01:11:32.354 --> 01:11:34.456 align:middle line:84%
And I want to warn
you about those

01:11:34.456 --> 01:11:36.058 align:middle line:84%
partly because you
guys are all going

01:11:36.058 --> 01:11:37.960 align:middle line:84%
to scatter and work
on whatever languages

01:11:37.960 --> 01:11:39.695 align:middle line:90%
it is you found to work on.

01:11:39.695 --> 01:11:41.196 align:middle line:84%
And I want you to
be prepared if you

01:11:41.196 --> 01:11:43.632 align:middle line:84%
encounter some of these types
of morphology out there.

01:11:43.632 --> 01:11:44.800 align:middle line:90%
there Is another kind.

01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:48.037 align:middle line:84%
O'odham, which is
a language spoken

01:11:48.037 --> 01:11:50.272 align:middle line:84%
by the traditional
owners of the area

01:11:50.272 --> 01:11:53.809 align:middle line:84%
around Tucson, Arizona, the
Tohono O'odham, the desert

01:11:53.809 --> 01:11:54.777 align:middle line:90%
people.

01:11:54.777 --> 01:11:55.377 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:11:55.377 --> 01:11:55.978 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE].

01:11:55.978 --> 01:11:57.279 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN W. RICHARDS: Oh, cool.

01:11:57.279 --> 01:12:01.750 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, this is the
language of your place.

01:12:01.750 --> 01:12:04.219 align:middle line:84%
So it has what are called
imperfect and perfect forms

01:12:04.219 --> 01:12:06.055 align:middle line:84%
of the verb, which you
can think of as being

01:12:06.055 --> 01:12:08.724 align:middle line:84%
kind of like present
tense and past tense.

01:12:08.724 --> 01:12:11.480 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
these, we're kind

01:12:11.480 --> 01:12:14.063 align:middle line:84%
of cheating because I've got the
name of this type of morpheme

01:12:14.063 --> 01:12:15.531 align:middle line:90%
up there at the top.

01:12:15.531 --> 01:12:18.567 align:middle line:84%
If you look at
these, it turns out

01:12:18.567 --> 01:12:20.235 align:middle line:84%
if you study a
lot about O'odham,

01:12:20.235 --> 01:12:22.705 align:middle line:84%
you can convince yourself--
so if you look at them,

01:12:22.705 --> 01:12:25.074 align:middle line:84%
you can see the imperfect
forms are a little bit longer

01:12:25.074 --> 01:12:26.642 align:middle line:90%
than the perfect forms.

01:12:26.642 --> 01:12:30.612 align:middle line:84%
So the perfect forms are things
like "neo" and "nei" and "hin."

01:12:30.612 --> 01:12:34.116 align:middle line:84%
And the imperfect forms are
forms like "neok" and "neid"

01:12:34.116 --> 01:12:37.853 align:middle line:90%
and "hink."

01:12:37.853 --> 01:12:41.623 align:middle line:84%
So your first hope might be that
the imperfect versions involve

01:12:41.623 --> 01:12:45.394 align:middle line:84%
suffixes, that you're starting
with the perfect versions

01:12:45.394 --> 01:12:48.263 align:middle line:90%
and adding suffixes.

01:12:48.263 --> 01:12:50.466 align:middle line:84%
But if you look at
these verbs, you

01:12:50.466 --> 01:12:54.036 align:middle line:84%
can hopefully convince
yourself that that's hopeless.

01:12:54.036 --> 01:12:56.889 align:middle line:84%
The suffix is just
kind of-- the consonant

01:12:56.889 --> 01:13:01.276 align:middle line:84%
that's at the end just varies
a lot from verb to verb.

01:13:01.276 --> 01:13:02.945 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound right?

01:13:02.945 --> 01:13:05.781 align:middle line:84%
So in fact, the going
hypothesis about what's

01:13:05.781 --> 01:13:08.751 align:middle line:84%
going on in O'odham is that
this is a linguistically very

01:13:08.751 --> 01:13:10.652 align:middle line:90%
unusual kind of morpheme.

01:13:10.652 --> 01:13:11.920 align:middle line:90%
It's called truncation.

01:13:11.920 --> 01:13:14.556 align:middle line:84%
The way you form the
perfect form of the verb

01:13:14.556 --> 01:13:16.658 align:middle line:84%
is to take the imperfect
form of the verb

01:13:16.658 --> 01:13:20.329 align:middle line:84%
and remove its last
consonant, whatever it is.

01:13:20.329 --> 01:13:23.065 align:middle line:90%
So it's called truncation.

01:13:23.065 --> 01:13:24.266 align:middle line:90%
You're not adding something.

01:13:24.266 --> 01:13:26.668 align:middle line:90%
You're subtracting something.

01:13:26.668 --> 01:13:29.738 align:middle line:84%
Morphology usually doesn't
subtract, it usually adds.

01:13:29.738 --> 01:13:36.378 align:middle line:84%
But this seems to be what's
happening in O'odham.

01:13:36.378 --> 01:13:39.548 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
other things-- tone.

01:13:39.548 --> 01:13:43.752 align:middle line:84%
So there are tonal
languages in the world.

01:13:43.752 --> 01:13:46.155 align:middle line:84%
If you've had the interaction
with Mandarin, for example,

01:13:46.155 --> 01:13:48.757 align:middle line:84%
you've heard of tonal
languages, languages in which

01:13:48.757 --> 01:13:51.393 align:middle line:84%
the pitch of your voice
makes has an effect

01:13:51.393 --> 01:13:54.263 align:middle line:84%
on the meaning of the
words that you're saying.

01:13:54.263 --> 01:13:57.800 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages
out there in which there

01:13:57.800 --> 01:14:00.169 align:middle line:90%
are morphemes that are tones.

01:14:00.169 --> 01:14:05.240 align:middle line:84%
So Dinka, which is a Nilotic
language spoken in South Sudan,

01:14:05.240 --> 01:14:08.115 align:middle line:84%
there's a popular
Dinka name, "Bol,"

01:14:08.115 --> 01:14:10.512 align:middle line:90%
which is a low-tone noun.

01:14:10.512 --> 01:14:13.816 align:middle line:84%
It can be inflected for
case, so to indicate

01:14:13.816 --> 01:14:15.918 align:middle line:84%
that it is the possessor
of another noun,

01:14:15.918 --> 01:14:19.321 align:middle line:84%
for example, if you want to say
things like "Bol's brother."

01:14:19.321 --> 01:14:22.391 align:middle line:84%
But what you do is to
change the tone of "Bol."

01:14:22.391 --> 01:14:25.260 align:middle line:84%
So instead of just being
"Bol," it's now "Bol,"

01:14:25.260 --> 01:14:27.362 align:middle line:90%
so it's a falling tone.

01:14:27.362 --> 01:14:32.334 align:middle line:84%
So Bol's brother is "manh
e Bol," so brother of Bol,

01:14:32.334 --> 01:14:34.903 align:middle line:90%
and "Bol" changes its tone.

01:14:34.903 --> 01:14:36.438 align:middle line:84%
There was an NBA
basketball player

01:14:36.438 --> 01:14:38.540 align:middle line:84%
for a while who was
Dinka, Manute Bol.

01:14:38.540 --> 01:14:45.380 align:middle line:84%
It's apparently a
really common name.

01:14:45.380 --> 01:14:47.649 align:middle line:84%
We spent a semester
trying to understand

01:14:47.649 --> 01:14:50.886 align:middle line:84%
Dinka in a graduate-level
Field Methods class.

01:14:50.886 --> 01:14:54.323 align:middle line:84%
Most Dinka morphology is
tones, and vowel length,

01:14:54.323 --> 01:14:55.557 align:middle line:90%
and other kinds of things.

01:14:55.557 --> 01:14:57.392 align:middle line:84%
We were always
deathly afraid that we

01:14:57.392 --> 01:14:59.027 align:middle line:84%
were missing half
of the morphemes

01:14:59.027 --> 01:15:01.196 align:middle line:84%
because they're
quite hard to hear.

01:15:01.196 --> 01:15:03.084 align:middle line:84%
And then-- this
is the last thing

01:15:03.084 --> 01:15:08.604 align:middle line:84%
before I let you go-- there
are arguably cases of morphemes

01:15:08.604 --> 01:15:10.458 align:middle line:90%
that are not pronounced at all.

01:15:10.458 --> 01:15:12.708 align:middle line:84%
So there are words like
"cat," and "dog," and "sheep,"

01:15:12.708 --> 01:15:14.843 align:middle line:84%
and there are plural
morphemes like "-s"

01:15:14.843 --> 01:15:17.179 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe the
plural of "sheep"

01:15:17.179 --> 01:15:18.547 align:middle line:90%
also has a plural morpheme.

01:15:18.547 --> 01:15:22.684 align:middle line:84%
It's just that that plural
morpheme is pronounced--

01:15:22.684 --> 01:15:25.387 align:middle line:84%
You can't tell, but
I'm opening my mouth.

01:15:25.387 --> 01:15:27.789 align:middle line:84%
So maybe there
are morphemes that

01:15:27.789 --> 01:15:31.520 align:middle line:84%
have no pronunciation at
all, they're just morphemes.

01:15:31.520 --> 01:15:32.895 align:middle line:84%
You shouldn't
necessarily believe

01:15:32.895 --> 01:15:35.364 align:middle line:84%
that just looking at this,
but it's a possibility

01:15:35.364 --> 01:15:38.166 align:middle line:84%
that we might want
to take seriously.

01:15:38.166 --> 01:15:40.002 align:middle line:90%
All right, good.

01:15:40.002 --> 01:15:44.039 align:middle line:84%
I think this is probably
a good stopping point.

01:15:44.039 --> 01:15:47.109 align:middle line:84%
So just to summarize,
and then I'll

01:15:47.109 --> 01:15:48.830 align:middle line:84%
let you go-- for
every morpheme, we're

01:15:48.830 --> 01:15:51.413 align:middle line:84%
going to want to indicate what's
it sound, what's its meaning.

01:15:51.413 --> 01:15:53.382 align:middle line:84%
Is it a bound morpheme
or a free morpheme?

01:15:53.382 --> 01:15:55.717 align:middle line:84%
And if it's bound, what kind
of bound morpheme is it?

01:15:55.717 --> 01:15:59.121 align:middle line:84%
Is it a prefix, a suffix, an
infix, any of these other kinds

01:15:59.121 --> 01:15:59.922 align:middle line:90%
of things?

01:15:59.922 --> 01:16:01.323 align:middle line:84%
All kinds of things
you'd want to understand

01:16:01.323 --> 01:16:02.824 align:middle line:84%
if you want to
understand everything

01:16:02.824 --> 01:16:04.927 align:middle line:84%
about the morphology
of a language.

01:16:04.927 --> 01:16:08.150 align:middle line:90%
We'll pick it up here next time.